{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 91 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "aa2cec51dd54179cf9ea1ba7759cf5cf9ed772f87d7334daeddb249cfe710e74" :edb/db-before-t 90 :edb/db-after-t 91 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533403 50 #inst "2026-10-10T07:44:44.957Z" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186054853 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275173 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186054853 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275268 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186059657 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275463 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186059657 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275477 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186059858 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275162 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186059858 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275164 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186059858 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275165 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186059858 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275166 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186059858 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275167 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186059858 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275168 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186059858 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275169 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186060066 1068 #edb/ref 17592186056724 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186060066 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274892 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186060066 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186060066 1071 "The vector $a$ has magnitude $3$, the vector $b$ has magnitude $4$, and the angle between the vectors is $0^{∘}$. What is $a \\cdot b$?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186060066 1071 "The vector $a$ has magnitude $3,$ the vector $b$ has magnitude $4,$ and the angle between the vectors is ${0}^{∘}.$ What is $a\\times b?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186060066 1074 "Suppose $a$ and $b$ are nonzero vectors. Then, $a\\times b=0$ if and only if $a∥b.$\n\nSince the angle between $a$ and $b$ is ${0}^{∘},$ our vectors must be parallel. \n\nTherefore, $a\\times b=0.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186060067 1068 #edb/ref 17592186056730 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186060067 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275060 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186060067 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186060067 1071 "The vector $a$ has magnitude $3$. What is $a \\cdot a$?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186060067 1071 "The vector $a$ has magnitude $3.$ What is $a\\times a?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186060067 1074 "Suppose $a$ and $b$ are nonzero vectors. Then, $a\\times b=0$ if and only if $a∥b.$\n\nNotice that any vector is parallel to itself. So, we have $a∥a.$\n\nTherefore, $a\\times a=0.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064252 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275157 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064252 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275158 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064257 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275376 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064257 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275381 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064257 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275383 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064258 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275191 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064258 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275354 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064258 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275355 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064258 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275356 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064258 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275357 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064258 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275358 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064322 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275315 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064322 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275316 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064322 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275317 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064322 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275318 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064327 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275350 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064327 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275351 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064514 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061284 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186064514 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274848 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064514 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064514 1071 "$\\int 6x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064514 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int6xe^{4x}dx$?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186064514 1074 "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int 6x{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=6x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=6 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{4x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 6x\\cdot {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =6x\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4x}-\\int \\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4x}\\cdot 6\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{3}{2}\\int {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3x{e}^{4x}}{2}-\\frac{3{e}^{4x}}{8}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064515 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061290 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186064515 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274879 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064515 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064515 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int2x\\sin (x)dx$." 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186064515 1071 "Calculate $\\int 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064515 1074 "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064518 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061308 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186064518 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274673 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064518 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064518 1071 "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x-1, & x<1 \\\\ 2-x, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)?$\n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=f(1)$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064518 1071 "Consider the following function:\n$f(x) = {2x - 1,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 1 \\\\ 2 - x,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 1$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)$?\n\n1. $f(x)$ is defined at $x = 1$\n2. $lim_(x → 1^{-})f(x) = lim_(x → 1^{+})f(x)$\n3. $lim_(x → 1)f(x) = f(1)$\n4. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x = 1$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186064518 1074 "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the second branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(1)=2-(1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(2x-1) \\\\  & =2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(2-x) \\\\  & =2-(1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ exists:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=1\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=1=f(1).$  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since all statements I-III are true, according to the continuity test, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064520 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061320 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186064520 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274975 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064520 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064520 1071 "Calculate $F(3)$ where $F(x) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{t}dt$." 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186064520 1071 "Calculate $F(3)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064520 1074 "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF(3)={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({3}^{3/2}-{0}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(({3}^{3})^{1/2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{27} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064521 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061326 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186064521 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274750 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064521 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064521 1071 "Calculate $F(\\frac{π}{2})$ where $F(x) = \\displaystyle \\int_{π/4}^{x}2\\cos tdt$." 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186064521 1071 "Calculate $F\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{x}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064521 1074 "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =2(1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =2-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064522 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061332 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186064522 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274863 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064522 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064522 1071 "![](images/21/21c86a5542f6bac4cd53c9d64d7c24a90ea6e901bd58e0204f38a91c2835e3aa.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y = f(x)$ is shown above. Given $g(t) = \\displaystyle \\int_{-3}^{t}f(x)dx$, determine the value of $g(3)$." 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186064522 1071 "![](images/21/21c86a5542f6bac4cd53c9d64d7c24a90ea6e901bd58e0204f38a91c2835e3aa.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-3}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064522 1074 "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b14fbfe336923b44e4e4f3660bef7c6be97ada82ea07c4523a3f25f2905f4fd6.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-3,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a triangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [0,3],$  for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a square.  This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-3,0],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [0,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{9}{2}+9=\\frac{27}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064523 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061338 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186064523 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274931 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064523 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064523 1071 "![](images/d4/d41d681a2f0e7a32400d863e60294efc9864ff6245de0e36515986da656ddbd2.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y = f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a quarter-circle of radius $3$. Given $g(t) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{t}f(x)dx$, determine the value of $g(5)$." 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186064523 1071 "![](images/d4/d41d681a2f0e7a32400d863e60294efc9864ff6245de0e36515986da656ddbd2.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a quarter-circle of radius $3.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(5).$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064523 1074 "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=5,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(5)={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](images/7d/7db30a3be837d9303ce4fca0b5a95466a74fe53f1ce81e6fa0febe2ef00625b5.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [0,2],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a triangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [2,5],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a quarter-circle cut out of a rectangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(5)={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [0,2],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [2,5],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the quarter-circle cut out of a rectangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =12-\\frac{9}{4}\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(5) & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =4+(12-\\frac{9}{4}\\pi ) \\\\  & =16-\\frac{9}{4}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064526 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061356 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186064526 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275006 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064526 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064526 1071 "![](images/24/24d83be9fed627018111aff41b976881c98c6c58b2ceeb0ff11c033f9926afd3.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y = f(x)$. The point $A$ has coordinates $(3, 2)$ and lies on $y = f(x)$. Given that $(2, 3)$ lies on $y = f^{-1}(x)$, what is $(f^{-1})^{′}(2)$?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186064526 1071 "![](images/24/24d83be9fed627018111aff41b976881c98c6c58b2ceeb0ff11c033f9926afd3.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(3,2)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(2,3)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064526 1074 "Since $(2,3)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=3.$\nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [1,5]$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064527 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061362 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186064527 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274957 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064527 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064527 1071 "$\\int \\frac{2}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186064527 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{2}{x + 1}dx$?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186064527 1074 "First, let $u=x+1.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068741 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275323 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068741 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275325 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068741 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275326 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068766 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275153 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068829 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275156 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275126 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068831 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275127 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068831 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275155 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068846 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275413 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068846 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275414 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068846 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275415 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068846 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275416 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068846 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275417 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068846 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275418 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068847 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275411 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068847 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275412 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068848 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275420 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068848 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275421 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068848 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275422 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068848 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275423 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068848 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275424 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068848 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275425 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068848 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275427 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068848 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275428 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068850 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275328 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068850 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275329 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068850 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275330 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068850 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275331 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068850 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275429 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068850 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275456 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068850 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275457 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186068887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275180 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186069074 1068 #edb/ref 17592186065847 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186069074 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274790 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186069074 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186069074 1071 "Calculate $\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186069074 1071 "Calculate $\\inte^{-x}\\cos (3x)dx$." 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186069074 1074 "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})(-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})(-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}J \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}I) \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9}-\\frac{1}{9}I \\\\ \\frac{10}{9}I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9} \\\\ \\frac{10}{9}I & =\\frac{3{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186069084 1068 #edb/ref 17592186065907 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186069084 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274757 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186069084 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186069084 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186069084 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2x}{e^{y}}$." 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186069084 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & ={x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n{e}^{y}={x}^{2}+C.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186069085 1068 #edb/ref 17592186065913 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186069085 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274817 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186069085 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186069085 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\,$ for $\\,|y|<1.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186069085 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\sqrt{1 - y^{2}}$ for $\\mid y \\mid < 1$." 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186069085 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y & =x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y=x+C.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186069087 1068 #edb/ref 17592186065925 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186069087 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274803 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186069087 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186069087 1071 "Separate the variables in the following differential equation:\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{xy^{2}}{x + 1}$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186069087 1071 "Separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{x+1}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186069087 1074 "A differentiable equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differentiable equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(\\frac{x}{x+1})\\cdot {y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{x+1}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186069090 1068 #edb/ref 17592186065943 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186069090 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275052 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186069090 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186069090 1071 "Separate the variables in the following differential equation:\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = e^{x + y}$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186069090 1071 "Separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x+y}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186069090 1074 "A differentiable equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differentiable equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{y}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x},\n$$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$$\n{e}^{-y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186073721 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275146 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186078396 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275319 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186078396 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275332 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186078396 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275334 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186078396 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275335 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186078396 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275336 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186078397 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275161 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186078605 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275160 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186078748 1068 #edb/ref 17592186075334 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186078748 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274862 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186078748 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186078748 1071 "![](images/82/824f039d5f015a2cd2165cfd469c44e7f3e5a0cda6416964ec91d57de4ca06eb.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y = f(x)$ above, find the value of\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{-3}^{4}f(x)dx$." 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186078748 1071 "![](images/82/824f039d5f015a2cd2165cfd469c44e7f3e5a0cda6416964ec91d57de4ca06eb.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186078748 1074 "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,1),$ the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [1,3],$ and the line segment for $x\\in [3,4].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,1).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =b\\cdot h \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-4.\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,3].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle of radius of $1,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}-{A}_{3} \\\\  & =-4+\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186078749 1068 #edb/ref 17592186075340 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186078749 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275009 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186078749 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186078749 1071 "A thermometer is placed into a water bath and displays a reading of $50^{∘}C$. The thermometer is then transferred to a $10^{∘}C$ water bath, and its reading begins to decrease. After $5$ minutes, the thermometer reading is $40^{∘}C$. To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the thermometer to register a reading of $30^{∘}C$?\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t > 0$ is the time, in minutes, since the thermometer was transferred into the $10^{∘}C$ water bath.*" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186078749 1071 "A thermometer is placed into a water bath and displays a reading of ${50}^{∘}C.$ The thermometer is then transferred to a ${10}^{∘}C$ water bath, and its reading begins to decrease. After $5$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${40}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the thermometer to register a reading of ${30}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the thermometer was transferred into the ${10}^{∘}C$ water bath.*" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186078749 1074 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={10}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-10).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-10} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-10} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-10| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-10>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-10 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-10 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-10 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =10+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${50}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=50,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50 & =10+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 50 & =10+K \\\\ K & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=10+40{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $5$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${40}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(5)=40,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40 & =10+40{e}^{-5k} \\\\ 30 & =40{e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & ={e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & =-5k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the thermometer reads ${30}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =30 \\\\ 10+40{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =30 \\\\ 40{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =20 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the thermometer will read ${30}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})}{-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}≈12\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute." 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186078750 1068 #edb/ref 17592186075346 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186078750 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274703 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186078750 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186078750 1071 "The temperature $T(t)$, in degrees Celsius, of a cup of coffee can be modeled by the differential equation\n$\\frac{dT}{dt} =-0.05(T - 25)$\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the coffee is $100^{∘}C$, calculate the temperature of the coffee after $6$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186078750 1071 "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a cup of coffee can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.05(T-25)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the coffee is ${100}^{∘}C,$ calculate the temperature of the coffee after $6$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186078750 1074 "We're told that the temperature of the cup of coffee is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.05(T-25).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-0.05\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-\\int 0.05\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-25| & =-0.05t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-25>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-25 & ={e}^{-0.05t+C} \\\\ T-25 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.05t} \\\\ T-25 & =K{e}^{-0.05t} \\\\ T & =25+K{e}^{-0.05t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the coffee was ${100}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=100,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =25+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 100 & =25+K \\\\ K & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=25+75{e}^{-0.05t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the coffee after $t=6$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(6)=25+75{e}^{-0.05\\cdot 6}≈{81}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083201 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275296 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083201 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275298 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083201 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275300 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083206 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275273 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083206 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275274 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083206 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275275 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083248 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275197 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083248 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275208 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083249 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275333 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083249 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275338 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083249 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275339 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083250 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275343 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083308 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275292 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083308 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275320 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083308 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275321 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083350 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275271 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083350 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275272 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083350 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275301 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083350 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275302 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083546 1068 #edb/ref 17592186080141 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186083546 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274825 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083546 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083546 1071 "${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083546 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{∞}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x^{9}}}dx$?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186083546 1074 "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{x}^{-9/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{2}{7}{x}^{-7/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{7}}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{a}^{7}}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{7}}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083547 1068 #edb/ref 17592186080147 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186083547 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274710 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083547 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083547 1071 "Which of the following gives the length of the curve $y = \\ln (\\csc x)$ on $[1, 3]$?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186083547 1071 "Which of the following gives the length of the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)$ on $[1,3]?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083547 1074 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x),$ $a=1,$ and $b=3.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}|\\operatorname{csc}⁡x|\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote, that we use $|\\operatorname{csc}⁡x|=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$, which is valid due to that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x>0$ on $[1,3].$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083553 1068 #edb/ref 17592186080183 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186083553 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274967 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083553 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083553 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,,\\,t\\in (0,\\pi ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186083553 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n$x = \\ln (\\sin t), y = \\frac{1}{\\sin t}, t ∈ (0, π)$.\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t = \\frac{π}{2}$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C$." 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186083553 1074 "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]=\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0, \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /2} & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(-1)\\,}=1\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186087664 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275337 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186087666 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275375 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186087667 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275441 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186087669 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275172 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186087706 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275256 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186087706 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275257 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186087706 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275258 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186087706 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275259 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186087748 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275419 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186087788 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275171 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186087792 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275410 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186087866 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275159 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186087866 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275195 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186087866 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275196 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186087866 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275209 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186087892 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275112 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186087892 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275113 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186087892 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275118 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186087893 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275114 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186087893 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275116 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186087894 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275115 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186087894 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275117 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186088014 1068 #edb/ref 17592186084788 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186088014 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275048 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186088014 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186088014 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+y{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186088014 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{3y^{3}}{xy^{2} + yx^{2}}$.\n\nIf the transformation $y = v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186088014 1074 "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+y{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{3(vx)^{3}}{x(vx)^{2}+(vx){x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{3{v}^{3}{x}^{3}}{v{x}^{3}(v+1)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{3{v}^{3}\\cancel{{x}^{3}}}{v\\cancel{{x}^{3}}(v+1)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{v}^{2}}{v+1}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{v}^{2}}{v+1}-\\frac{v(v+1)}{v+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{v}^{2}-{v}^{2}-v}{v+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2{v}^{2}-v}{v+1} \\\\ (\\frac{v+1}{2{v}^{2}-v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186088022 1068 #edb/ref 17592186084836 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186088022 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275013 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186088022 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186088022 1071 "The number of particles $N(t)$ in a radioactive substance after $t$ years can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-3N.\n$$\n\nIf there were $8\\,500$ particles initially, calculate the number of particles remaining after $2$ years. Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186088022 1071 "The number of particles $N(t)$ in a radioactive substance after $t$ years can be modeled by the differential equation\n$\\frac{dN}{dt} =-3N$.\nIf there were $8500$ particles initially, calculate the number of particles remaining after $2$ years. Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186088022 1074 "We model the number of particles in the substance using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-3N.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there were $8\\,500$ particles initially, so we have ${N}_{0}=8\\,500.$\n\nTherefore, the number of particles $N$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nN(t)=8\\,500\\,{e}^{-3t}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of particles when $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(2) & =8\\,500\\,{e}^{-3\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =8\\,500\\,{e}^{-6} \\\\  & ≈21.069\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that $21$ particles remain." 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186092270 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275263 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186092270 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275264 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186092274 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275207 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186092322 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275152 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186092379 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275266 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186092379 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275267 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186092379 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275269 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186092382 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275270 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186092570 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089561 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186092570 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274990 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186092570 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186092570 1071 "Which of the following gives the total area enclosed by graph of the polar curve $r = 1 + (\\cos (θ))/(4)$ for $0 \\le θ \\le 2π$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186092570 1071 "Which of the following gives the total area enclosed by the graph of the polar curve $r=1+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )}{4}$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186092570 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=1+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )}{4}$, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=2\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(1+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )}{4})}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186092581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089627 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186092581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274880 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186092581 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186092581 1071 "![](images/80/80577a585741748702b61596db84983592ecbc6c01fca78af033bf82dbe62ad2.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x = e^{t}, y = e^{t}\\sin t$\nfrom $t =-3$ to $t = 1$ (shown above)?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186092581 1071 "![](images/80/80577a585741748702b61596db84983592ecbc6c01fca78af033bf82dbe62ad2.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x={e}^{t},\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\nfrom $t=-3$ to $t=1$ (shown above)?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186092581 1074 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{2t}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186096653 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275093 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186096697 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275094 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186096697 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275095 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186096811 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275362 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186096813 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275366 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186096840 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275179 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186100840 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275163 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186100841 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275170 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186100856 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275193 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186100857 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275192 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186100857 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275194 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186100895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275359 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186100895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275360 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186100895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275372 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186100896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275361 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186100896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275373 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186100896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275407 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186100896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275408 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186100899 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275109 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186100899 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275110 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186100899 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275111 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186100934 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275352 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186100934 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275353 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186100939 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275322 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186100939 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275327 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186100939 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275340 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186100943 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275324 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186100943 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275341 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186101030 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275286 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186101204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098529 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186101204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274691 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186101204 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186101204 1071 "What is the coefficient of $(x + 1)^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x) = e^{3x}$ at $x =-1$?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186101204 1071 "What is the coefficient of $(x+1)^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)={e}^{3x}$ at $x=-1?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186101204 1074 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=-1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(-1)+{f}^{′}(-1)(x+1)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-1)}{2}(x+1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $(x+1)^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(-1).\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(-1)=9{e}^{3(-1)}=9{e}^{-3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x+1)^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{″}(-1)}{2} & =\\frac{9{e}^{-3}}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186101206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098541 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186101206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274805 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186101206 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186101206 1071 "What is the coefficient of $(x + π)^{3}$ in the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x) = \\sin x$ about $x = - π$?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186101206 1071 "What is the coefficient of $(x+\\pi )^{3}$ in the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ about $x=-\\pi ?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186101206 1074 "The coefficient of $(x+\\pi )^{3}$ is $\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{(3)}(-\\pi ).$ Let's compute the necessary derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{‴}(-\\pi ),$ which gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{‴}(-\\pi )=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{‴}(-\\pi )=\\frac{1}{3!}\\cdot 1=\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the coefficient of $(x+\\pi )^{3}$ is $\\frac{1}{6}.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186101213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098583 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186101213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274819 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186101213 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186101213 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t-2},\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2},\\,t\\ne 2.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186101213 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n$x = \\frac{1}{t - 2}, y = t^{2}, t \\ne 2$." 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186101213 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{t-2} \\\\ x(t-2) & =1 \\\\ t-2 & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{x}+2 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1+2x}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t=\\frac{1+2x}{x}$ into our expression for $y={t}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne 2$ and $x=\\frac{1}{t-2},$ we must have $x\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186101214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098589 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186101214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274661 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186101214 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186101214 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = 2\\sin t - 1, y = 5\\cos t + 4, t ∈ [0, 2π)$." 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186101214 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1,\\,\\,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186101214 1074 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{y-4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186101215 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098595 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186101215 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274654 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186101215 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186101215 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186101215 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = 2\\cos t, y = 3\\sin t, t ∈ [0, 2π)$." 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186101215 1074 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186105726 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275377 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186105861 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275236 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186105862 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275237 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186105862 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275238 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186105862 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275239 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186105867 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275478 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186105867 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275482 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186105899 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275185 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186105899 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275186 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186105899 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275187 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186105899 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275188 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186105899 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275189 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186105899 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275190 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186105900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275181 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186105900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275182 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186105900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275183 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186105900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275184 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186106059 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102210 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186106059 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274996 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186106059 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186106059 1071 "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{8}{3x}$?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186106059 1071 "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{8}{3x}?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186106059 1074 "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $\\frac{8}{3}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{3x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{8}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{3x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186106060 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102216 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186106060 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274660 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186106060 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186106060 1071 "![](images/88/8835c085ca34d59f5c79786e12ed67cb33ccfdcfefd706dcc83ca2be0239670f.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2},\\, & 0<x<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186106060 1071 "![](images/88/8835c085ca34d59f5c79786e12ed67cb33ccfdcfefd706dcc83ca2be0239670f.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n$f(x) = {3x^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}0 < x < 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f(x) \\ge 0$ for all $x$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}f(x)dx = 1$\n3. $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186106060 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[{1}^{3}]-[{0}^{3}] \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119930 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275382 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119930 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275387 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119930 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275388 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119930 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275389 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119930 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275390 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119930 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275391 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119930 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275392 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119930 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275393 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119930 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275394 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119932 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275363 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119932 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275364 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119932 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275365 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119932 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275367 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119932 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275395 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119933 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275369 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119933 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275396 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119933 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275397 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119933 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275398 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119933 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275399 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119933 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275400 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119934 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275370 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119934 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275401 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119934 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275402 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119934 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275403 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119934 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275404 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119934 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275405 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119938 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275450 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119938 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275451 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119938 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275452 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119938 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275453 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119938 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275454 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119938 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275455 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119938 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275474 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119938 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275475 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186119938 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275476 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186120152 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117088 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186120152 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274992 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186120152 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186120152 1071 "Let $L$ be the line in $R^{3}$ that passes through the points $(-2, 2,-2)$ and $(3,-4, 2)$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n2. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n3. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n4. $L$ is **not** a subspace of $R^{3}$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186120152 1071 "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(-2,2,-2)$ and $(3,-4,2).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186120152 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{5}=\\frac{y-2}{-6}=\\frac{z+2}{4}.\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since \n\n$$\n\\frac{(0)+2}{5}\\ne \\frac{(0)-2}{-6}\\ne \\frac{(0)+2}{4}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the vectors $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHowever, the point $(1,-2,0)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n\n$$\n\\frac{(1)+2}{5}\\ne \\frac{(-2)-2}{-6}\\ne \\frac{(0)+2}{4}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186120154 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117100 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186120154 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274734 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186120154 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186120154 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{1}-2{a}_{2}=0.$\n- ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n- The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent." 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186120154 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $a_{1} - 2a_{2} = 0$.\n2. $x_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2} = 0$ only if $x_{1} = x_{2} = 0$.\n3. The set ${a_{1}, a_{2}}$ is linearly independent." 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186120154 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}-2{a}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186120155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117106 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186120155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274925 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186120155 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186120155 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-2{a}_{3}=0.$\n- ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent." 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186120155 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}, a_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $a_{1} + a_{2} - 2a_{3} = 0$.\n2. $x_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2} + x_{3}a_{3} = 0$ only if $x_{1} = x_{2} = x_{3} = 0$.\n3. The set ${a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}}$ is linearly independent." 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186120155 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-2{a}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+1-2 \\\\ 2+0-2 \\\\ 0+2-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ for ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=1$ and ${x}_{3}=-2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186120158 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117124 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186120158 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274768 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186120158 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186120158 1071 "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $x - y - z = 1$ in $R^{3}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n2. $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n3. $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n4. $Π$ is **not** a subspace of $R^{3}$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186120158 1071 "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $x-y-z=1$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186120158 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ x-y-1\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x-y-1 \\\\ (0) & =(0)-(0)-(1) \\\\ 0 & =-1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 0+0 \\\\ -1+0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x-y-1 \\\\ (-1) & =(1)-(0)-1 \\\\ -1 & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186120159 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117130 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186120159 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274676 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186120159 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186120159 1071 "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z = x - y$ in $R^{3}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n2. $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n3. $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n4. $Π$ is **not** a subspace of $R^{3}$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186120159 1071 "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=x-y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186120159 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ x-y\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=x-y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x-y \\\\ (0) & =(0)-(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}-{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}-{a}_{2})+({b}_{1}-{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})-({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x-y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k({a}_{1}-{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k{a}_{1}-k{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x-y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186120161 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117142 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186120161 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274888 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186120161 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186120161 1071 "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186120161 1071 "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$a = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, c = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, d = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186120161 1074 "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]=5\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1)=-30.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-30|=30.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186124642 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275459 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186124643 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275460 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186124643 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275461 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186124643 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275462 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186124800 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121367 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186124800 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274842 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186124800 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186124800 1071 "A University Campus has a total of $1500$ students.\nAt the beginning of the new semester at the University, flu affected $300$ students. After $10$ days, the number of students affected by the flu increases to $500$. To the nearest day, how long does it take for the flu-stricken campus population to reach $800$ students?\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of students affected by the flu, $P(t)$, increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t \\ge 0$ is the time, in days.*" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186124800 1071 "A University Campus has a total of $1\\,500$ students.\nAt the beginning of the new semester at the University, flu affected $300$ students. After $10$ days, the number of students affected by the flu increases to $500.$ To the nearest day, how long does it take for the flu-stricken campus population to reach $800$ students?\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of students affected by the flu, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time, in days.*" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186124800 1074 "The rate at which the campus population affected by the flu, $P(t),$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total campus population and its current population affected. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the total population of the campus, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=1\\,500$ students, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(1\\,500-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{1\\,500-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{1\\,500-P} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1\\,500-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $1\\,500-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,500-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 1\\,500-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 1\\,500-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =1\\,500-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial campus population affected by the flu was $300$ students. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=300,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =300 \\\\ 1\\,500-K{e}^{0} & =300 \\\\ 1\\,500-K & =300 \\\\ K & =1\\,200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=1\\,500-1\\,200{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $10$ days, the campus population affected by flu was $500$ students. So, applying the condition $P(10)=500,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =500 \\\\ 1\\,500-1\\,200{e}^{-10k} & =500 \\\\ 1\\,000 & =1\\,200{e}^{-10k} \\\\ {e}^{-10k} & =\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ -10k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{6}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when $800$ students are affected by the flu. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =800 \\\\ 1\\,500-1\\,200{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =800 \\\\ 700 & =1\\,200{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{7}{12} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{12}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{12}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that $800$ students will be affected by the flu after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{12})}{-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{6})}=29.562…≈30\\,\\text{days},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day." 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186124801 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121373 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186124801 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274849 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186124801 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186124801 1071 "![](images/96/9685260f754e0d6333de6f24ffbf3908c055d6e4fe2d3ffe47391410735c7df2.png)\n\nConsider the set $M = [- 2, 1)$ and the points $A$, $B$, and $C$, shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n2. $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n3. $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186124801 1071 "![](images/96/9685260f754e0d6333de6f24ffbf3908c055d6e4fe2d3ffe47391410735c7df2.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=[-2,1)$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186124801 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $A$ is not an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $A$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/39/39f7097f82f19d703208f9e4e8638e4d64aa417ad9cf4bc1f90092eb0c5e5639.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $B$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](images/3f/3ffa79d3052247aecd276c7794a498340acb66a1e74aeddcdd7800b1e9704506.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/f1/f116e950d99e0a00908c6c40a3c788cc450efa120843369e13f857cb084d8a1e.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only.'" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186124803 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121385 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186124803 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274942 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186124803 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186124803 1071 "Find the domain of the function $z = (\\ln (x + y))/(y)$." 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186124803 1071 "Find the domain of the function $z=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)}{y}.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186124803 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y>0 \\\\ y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x+y>0,\\,y\\ne 0}.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186124804 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121391 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186124804 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274799 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [1