in of the function $f(x, y) = \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} - 1}$?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186124806 1071 "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186124806 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1}.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves the $\\ge $ sign, the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ is part of the domain. Therefore, we draw a solid curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and shade the area *outside* the curve.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/ba/ba31bbc16c58860a4bc3c4d33ea1a61cb3d86d0e73d8aa6a67869b3e96a7fe32.png)" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129180 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275228 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129180 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275229 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129180 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275230 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129180 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275231 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129180 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275232 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129180 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275233 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129180 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275234 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129225 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275217 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129225 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275218 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129260 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275096 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129260 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275097 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129260 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275098 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129260 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275099 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129260 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275100 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129260 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275101 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275102 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275103 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275104 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275105 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275106 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275107 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275108 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129265 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275235 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129265 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275240 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129265 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275243 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129265 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275245 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129265 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275247 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129265 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275248 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126494 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186129504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274821 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129504 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129504 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume $V$ of a balloon** being inflated **increases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **square of the volume**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of volume.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{{V}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{V}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129506 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126506 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186129506 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274833 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129506 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129506 1071 "Find the value of the double sum\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{10}(1-{a}_{ij}-{b}_{ij})\n$$\n\ngiven that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{10}{a}_{ij}=10,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{10}{b}_{ij}=20.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129506 1071 "Find the value of the double sum\n\n$∑_(i = 1)^(5)∑_(j = 1)^(10)(1 - a_{ij} - b_{ij})$\n\ngiven that\n\n$∑_(i = 1)^(5)∑_(j = 1)^(10)a_{ij} = 10, ∑_(i = 1)^(5)∑_(j = 1)^(10)b_{ij} = 20$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186129506 1074 "The sum and constant multiple rules for double summations state that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({a}_{ij}+{b}_{ij}) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{ij}+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{b}_{ij}, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}k{a}_{ij} & =k{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{ij}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum rule to the given double summation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{10}(1-{a}_{ij}-{b}_{ij}) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{10}1-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{10}{a}_{ij}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{10}{b}_{ij} \\\\  & =({\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{10}1)-10-20 \\\\  & =({\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{10}1)-30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}1=mn,\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{10}1)-30 & =5\\cdot 10-30 \\\\  & =50-30 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{10}(1-{a}_{ij}-{b}_{ij})=20.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129508 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126518 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186129508 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274922 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129508 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129508 1071 "Find the value of the double sum\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{30}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{30}2{a}_{i}({b}_{j}-2)\n$$\n\ngiven that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{30}{a}_{i}=42,\\,{\\sum }_{j=1}^{30}{b}_{j}=70.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186129508 1071 "Find the value of the double sum\n\n$∑_(i = 1)^(30)∑_(j = 1)^(30)2a_{i}(b_{j} - 2)$\n\ngiven that\n\n$∑_(i = 1)^(30)a_{i} = 42, ∑_(j = 1)^(30)b_{j} = 70$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186129508 1074 "The product rule for double summations states that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{i}{b}_{j}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{a}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{b}_{j}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the product rule for double summations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{30}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{30}2{a}_{i}({b}_{j}-2) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{30}2{a}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{30}({b}_{j}-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the rules for single summations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{30}2{a}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{30}({b}_{j}-2) & =(2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{30}{a}_{i})\\cdot ({\\sum }_{j=1}^{30}{b}_{j}-{\\sum }_{j=1}^{30}2) \\\\  & =(2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{30}{a}_{i})\\cdot ({\\sum }_{j=1}^{30}{b}_{j}-2{\\sum }_{j=1}^{30}1) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 42)\\cdot (70-2\\cdot 30) \\\\  & =(84)\\cdot (70-60) \\\\  & =(84)\\cdot (10) \\\\  & =840\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186133794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275458 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186133795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275469 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186152164 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275200 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186152164 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275201 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186152164 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275202 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186152164 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275203 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186152165 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275198 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186152165 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275199 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186152194 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275409 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186157404 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275345 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186157404 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275346 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186157404 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275347 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186157404 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275348 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186157404 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275349 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186157404 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275368 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186161985 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275139 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186161985 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275140 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186162030 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275141 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186162030 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275142 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186162030 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275143 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186162030 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275144 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186162069 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275147 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186162069 