 ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1)?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186184522 1074 "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1):$\n\n![](images/9e/9e2e705cf17a5dca362c23a23e4d10067ff4390a3f9464dddb6745d78986c02a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1)$ is $x\\in [0,1].$ This means that $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1)$ is undefined outside this interval.\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1)$ is undefined." 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186184525 1068 #edb/ref 17592186181296 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186184525 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274731 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186184525 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186184525 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x))=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186184525 1071 "What is $lim_(x → - 1/2)\\tan (\\frac{1}{2}\\arccos (x))$?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186184525 1074 "![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186184537 1068 #edb/ref 17592186181368 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186184537 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274907 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186184537 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186184537 1071 "![](images/ef/ef5618f26d499eeffd693d1f0dc674761bcfd5762b9620044b295870983799a0.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $f$. Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x =-3$?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186184537 1071 "![](images/ef/ef5618f26d499eeffd693d1f0dc674761bcfd5762b9620044b295870983799a0.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $f.$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=-3?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186184537 1074 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-3$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(-3)+{f}^{′}(-3)(x+3)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-3)}{2}(x+3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(-3)<0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(-3)>0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=-3$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-3)<0,$ because the graph is concave down at $x=-3.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=-2+(x+3)-(x+3)^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186188268 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275260 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186188268 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275261 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186192722 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275287 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186192722 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275288 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186192722 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275289 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186192722 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275290 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186192722 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275291 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186192723 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275310 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186192723 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275311 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186192723 1038 #edb/ref 1759218627531