014 1071 "The function $f(x)=\\frac{9}{(x-2)^{2}}$ is defined for $x>2.$ Find ${f}^{-1}(x).$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186193014 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{9}{(x-2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{9}{(y-2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{9}{(y-2)^{2}} \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{x} \\\\ \\sqrt{(y-2)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{x}} \\\\ |y-2| & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{x}}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that for $y>2,$ we have\n\n$$\n|y-2|=y-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{x}}+2.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186193015 1068 #edb/ref 17592186189946 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186193015 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274771 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186193015 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186193015 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x) = \\frac{x - 6}{3 - 2x}$." 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186193015 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-6}{3-2x}.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186193015 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x-6}{3-2x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{y-6}{3-2y}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{y-6}{3-2y} \\\\ x(3-2y) & =y-6 \\\\ 3x-2xy & =y-6 \\\\ 3x+6 & =2xy+y \\\\ 3x+6 & =y(2x+1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3x+6}{2x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3x+6}{2x+1}.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186193017 1068 #edb/ref 17592186189958 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186193017 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275082 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186193017 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186193017 1071 "The function $f(x)$, defined as\n$f(x) = \\frac{x + k}{9 - x}, x \\ne 9$\nhas an inverse function given by\n$f^{-1}(x) = \\frac{9x + 7}{x + 1}, x \\ne - 1$.\nWhat is the value of $k$?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186193017 1071 "The function $f(x),$ defined as \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x+k}{9-x},\\,x\\ne 9\n$$\n\nhas an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9x+7}{x+1},\\,x\\ne -1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186193017 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x+k}{9-x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{y+k}{9-y}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{y+k}{9-y} \\\\ x(9-y) & =y+k \\\\ 9x-xy & =y+k \\\\ 9x-k & =xy+y \\\\ 9x-k & =y(x+1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{9x-k}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9x-k}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-7.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186193119 1068 #edb/ref 17592186190570 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186193119 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274829 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186193119 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186193119 1071 "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186193119 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{- ∞}^{0}5^{x/2}dx$?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186193119 1074 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{5}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{2\\cdot {5}^{x/2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{5}^{x/2}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{5}^{a/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a/2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{x/2}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{x/2}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x/2}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/14/141d1745dc939ca63155ca3313257f385af6d3c505c16bd63ba3b91e1e0ba64b.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186193120 1068 #edb/ref 17592186190576 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186193120 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275045 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186193120 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186193120 1071 "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{6t}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186193120 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{- ∞}^{1}\\frac{6t}{t^{4} + 3}dt$?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186193120 1074 "First, we substitute $u={t}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=2t\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=t\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $t\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={t}^{2}$ is as follows:\n\n| $t$ | $1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{6t}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=3{\\int }_{\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u=-3{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6t}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t & =-3{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{3}})]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})]-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/c8/c870dd367332455f2d4c35f85a188c64385a5195ca1058405a93f84c4ce31fd1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{6t}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})]-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186193139 1068 #edb/ref 17592186190690 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186193139 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274897 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186193139 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186193139 1071 "$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-x},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186193139 1071 "$f(x) = {e^{1 - x},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f(x) \\ge 0$ for all $x \\ge 1$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{∞}f(x)dx = 1$\n3. $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186193139 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 1$, we have $f(x)={e}^{1-x}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{1-x}]}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =0-(-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186193184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186190960 13194139533403 17592186045585 false] [17592186193184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274758 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186193184 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186193184 1071 "![](images/c2/c2452160f8af4651bdc78756b7c0df8402de185ba7a7bedbc1c1a676b342af97.png)\n\nConsider the set $A$, shown above. 