7592186045585 true] [17592186275090 1080 #uuid "fdfb59db-a83f-5c99-bc94-07a6f173c66c" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275091 1075 #edb/ref 17592186272950 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275091 1075 #edb/ref 17592186273293 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275091 1075 #edb/ref 17592186273488 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275091 1075 #edb/ref 17592186273811 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275091 1075 #edb/ref 17592186274084 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275091 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275091 1077 "selection" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275091 1079 #edb/ref 17592186274084 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275091 1080 #uuid "feffcd8a-4c9d-55f9-97f6-0cb4a9212f74" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275092 1075 #edb/ref 17592186273046 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275092 1075 #edb/ref 17592186273320 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275092 1075 #edb/ref 17592186273339 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275092 1075 #edb/ref 17592186273612 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275092 1075 #edb/ref 17592186274399 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275092 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275092 1077 "selection" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275092 1079 #edb/ref 17592186274399 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275092 1080 #uuid "ffdcc346-325e-5f90-b6ba-4c4488fb1963" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275093 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275034 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275093 1069 "q-142999" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275093 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275093 1071 "![](images/c6/c61b5aec221dd257c507fdaa8f2a04f0f29b158a6ea93afd38a310fc1150669d.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{QP}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{ST}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=c,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TP}=d.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{SQ}$ in terms of $a,b,c,$ and $d.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275093 1072 #uuid "0ff31a03-208b-5700-94d6-880e21a820f2" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275093 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $S$ to $Q$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{SQ}=\\overset{\\to }{ST}+\\overset{\\to }{TP}+\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/abd73b231ee79396e64c018ccfaed752efff4bedc053b1d9977761c283a3b994.png)\n\n  \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{ST}=b,\\,\\overset{\\to }{TP}=d,\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}=-a.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/c5/c560ab74e5d87c51adb19c287b1e75aa2ee112170f3e6361e1b5bc65c182dbda.png)\n\n   \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{SQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{ST}+\\overset{\\to }{TP}+\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =b+d-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275094 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274824 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275094 1069 "q-143001" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275094 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275094 1071 "![](images/67/67706db50684aef52269d20065caa21e4301834835e10e1fd3e456646ae2b32e.png)\n\nIn figure $ABCDEF$ shown above, which of the following are equal to the vector $\\overset{\\to }{DF}?$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{DC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB}+\\overset{\\to }{BF}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{FB}-\\overset{\\to }{DB}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{DE}+\\overset{\\to }{EF}$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275094 1072 #uuid "6378e369-5031-5af4-8366-576c75d82fa3" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275094 1074 "- For the $1$st expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB}+\\overset{\\to }{BF} & =(\\overset{\\to }{DC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB})+\\overset{\\to }{BF} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{DB}+\\overset{\\to }{BF} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{DF}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the $2$nd expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{FB}-\\overset{\\to }{DB} & =\\overset{\\to }{FB}+\\overset{\\to }{BD} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{FD} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{DF}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the $3$rd expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE}+\\overset{\\to }{EF} & =\\overset{\\to }{DF}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275095 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275036 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275095 1069 "q-143006" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275095 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275095 1071 "![](images/15/15f4bd7f8ecf8f61ce84858e33cdfa468b0de360fac19b0713b6b2dbc4af8d2e.png)\n\nIn figure $ABCDEF$ shown above, which of the following equalities are true?\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AB}-\\overset{\\to }{AF}=\\overset{\\to }{BF}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{DE}+\\overset{\\to }{EA}-\\overset{\\to }{BA}=\\overset{\\to }{DB}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BE}-\\overset{\\to }{DE}-\\overset{\\to }{CD}=\\overset{\\to }{CB}$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275095 1072 #uuid "32cdac5a-ca17-59c1-8641-8b3b47e09944" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275095 1074 "- Equality I is false. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}-\\overset{\\to }{AF} & =\\overset{\\to }{AB}+\\overset{\\to }{FA} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{FB} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{BF}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equality II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE}+\\overset{\\to }{EA}-\\overset{\\to }{BA} & =\\overset{\\to }{DE}+(\\overset{\\to }{EA}+\\overset{\\to }{AB}) \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{DE}+\\overset{\\to }{EB} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{DB}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equality III is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BE}-\\overset{\\to }{DE}-\\overset{\\to }{CD} & =(\\overset{\\to }{BE}+\\overset{\\to }{ED})+\\overset{\\to }{DC} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BD}+\\overset{\\to }{DC} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{CB}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275096 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274797 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275096 1069 "q-143029" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275096 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275096 1071 "The population $P$ of mosquitos in a particular region increases with time. The rate of change of the population is proportional to the square of the population. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the population of mosquitos at time $t?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275096 1072 #uuid "77296994-85cc-5c1d-b4fb-ea45337542b0" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275096 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **population** $P$ of mosquitos **increases** with time. The **rate** of change of the population of mosquitos is **proportional** to the **square of the population**.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *population* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *population squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}∝{P}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the population *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k{P}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275097 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274893 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275097 1069 "q-143031" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275097 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275097 1071 "The number of sales $S$ of a particular book increases with time. The rate of change of the number of sales is proportional to the square of the number of sales. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the number of sales of the book at time $t?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275097 1072 #uuid "022291b9-7de3-5769-a1c6-711e97510f12" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275097 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **number of sales** $S$ of a particular book **increases** with time. The **rate** of change of the number of sales is **proportional** to the **the square of the number of sales**.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *number of sales* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *number of sales squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}∝{S}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the number of sales *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=k{S}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275098 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274806 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275098 1069 "q-143035" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275098 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275098 1071 "During a certain chemical reaction, the volume of gas $G$ increases with time. The rate of change of the volume of gas is proportional to the square of the time $t,$ in seconds, since the reaction started. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of gas at time $t?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275098 1072 #uuid "71b2100f-b8f1-5ebd-9167-ed6423aec448" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275098 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*During a certain chemical reaction, the **volume** of gas $G$ **increases** with time. The **rate** of change of the volume of gas is **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t,$ in seconds.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume of gas* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *square of the time.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}∝{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of gas *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275099 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274951 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275099 1069 "q-143036" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275099 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275099 1071 "The velocity $v$ of a stone falling through some water to the bottom of a lake decreases with time. The rate of change of the stone's velocity is proportional to the square of the velocity. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the velocity at time $t?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275099 1072 #uuid "f6b52050-daad-5fe7-bea8-7d25b3613822" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275099 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **velocity** $v$ of a stone falling through some water to the bottom of a lake **decreases** with time. The **rate** of change of the stone's velocity is **proportional** to the **square of the velocity**.  *\n\nWe note the following: \n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *velocity* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *velocity squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}∝{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the velocity *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-k{v}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275100 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274657 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275100 1069 "q-143038" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275100 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275100 1071 "During a chemical reaction, the mass $M$ of a particular substance decreases with time. The rate of change of the substance's mass is proportional to the square of the mass. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the substance's mass at time $t?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275100 1072 #uuid "bd0bde68-c825-5fc5-9b25-364aaf1a5323" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275100 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*During a chemical reaction, the **mass** $M$ of a particular substance  **decreases** with time. The **rate** of change of the substance's mass is **proportional** to the **square of the mass**.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *mass* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *mass squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}∝{M}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the mass *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=-k{M}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275101 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274934 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275101 1069 "q-143039" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275101 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275101 1071 "Mia won some money in the lottery and is now spending it. The amount of money $M$ in her bank account decreases at a rate that's proportional to the square of the time $t,$ in days, since she won.\nWhat differential equation describes the rate of change of the amount of money $M$ at time $t?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275101 1072 #uuid "cddaa9ce-1cb6-5174-a6f2-b1cf405cc4b3" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275101 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **amount** of the money $M$ **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t,$ in days.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *amount* of money varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *square of the time.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}∝{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the amount *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=-k{t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275102 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274919 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275102 1069 "q-143056" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275102 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275102 1071 "The temperature $T$ of a chamber decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the temperature of the chamber. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the temperature $T?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275102 1072 #uuid "9a112fd6-7db0-5bcb-9456-2dfdfee150e3" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275102 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **temperature** $T$ of a chamber **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **temperature** $T$  of the chamber.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *temperature* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *temperature.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{T}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the temperature *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{T}.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275103 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274909 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275103 1069 "q-143058" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275103 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275103 1071 "The height $H$ of a tree increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the tree's height at that moment.\nWhat differential equation describes the rate of change of the height $H?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275103 1072 #uuid "84b0c1a4-e271-5034-a361-66c829b30871" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275103 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **height** $H$ of a tree **increases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **tree's height** $H$ at that moment. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *height* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *tree's height.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{H}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the height *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{H}.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275104 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275040 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275104 1069 "q-143061" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275104 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275104 1071 "The volume of water $V$ in a pool being filled increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of the volume of water in the pool at that moment. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of water $V$ in the pool?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275104 1072 #uuid "da6ceb8c-e4f9-5aca-9d84-9e6d61a0ff0a" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275104 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The  **volume of water** $V$  in a pool being filled **increases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the ** square of the volume of water** $V$ in the pool at that moment. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume of water* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the volume.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{{V}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of water *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{V}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275105 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274918 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275105 1069 "q-143077" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275105 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275105 1071 "In a controlled environment, the temperature $T$ of a food container decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of the temperature. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the temperature of the container $T?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275105 1072 #uuid "41368c60-4795-5758-bf61-d31f38f37b4a" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275105 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*In a controlled environment, the **temperature** $T$ of a food container **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **square of the temperature.** *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *temperature $T$* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the temperature $T.$* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{{T}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the temperature *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{T}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275106 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274707 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275106 1069 "q-143078" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275106 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275106 1071 "The intensity $I$ of a light source with a motion sensor increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of the distance $d$ from the source to the object detected by the sensor. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the intensity of the light source $I?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275106 1072 #uuid "08dff106-f96a-56cf-a1c9-2c911e731dc2" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275106 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **intensity** $I$ of a light source with a motion sensor **increases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **square of the distance** $d$ from the source to the object detected by the sensor.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *intensity* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the distance.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{{d}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the intensity *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{d}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275107 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274910 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275107 1069 "q-143081" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275107 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275107 1071 "The charge $C$ of a phone battery decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the time $t$ spent charging the smartphone. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the charge $C$ of the phone battery?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275107 1072 #uuid "8ee6d53c-a0fc-563a-989b-2d2f6587822e" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275107 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **charge** $C$ of a phone battery **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **time** $t$ spent charging the smartphone.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *charge* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *time.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{t}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the number *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{t}.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275108 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274804 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275108 1069 "q-143094" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275108 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275108 1071 "The speed $S$ of an object decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of the travel time $t.$ What differential equation describes the rate of change of the speed $S?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275108 1072 #uuid "0594774f-7b3e-5278-854b-b5d417c6c3ae" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275108 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **speed** $S$ of an object **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **square of the travel time** $t.$*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *speed* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the travel time.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the speed *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275109 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274823 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275109 1069 "q-14310" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275109 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275109 1071 "Find the specific solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+1$ given that  $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275109 1072 #uuid "c2219a64-4fca-5417-a4ef-4988c9db8d90" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275109 1074 "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(y) & =x+C \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+C).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1$ to find $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+C) \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ C & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+1$ that satisfies the condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275110 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274812 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275110 1069 "q-14312" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275110 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275110 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y{e}^{-x}$ that satisfies $y(0)={e}^{-1}.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275110 1072 #uuid "dd2fa0cf-e14d-5280-8a92-ce27abb0aa11" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275110 1074 "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,dx & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =-{e}^{-x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{-{e}^{-x}+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-{e}^{-x}+C} \\\\ y & =\\pm {e}^{-{e}^{-x}+C} \\\\ y & =K{e}^{-{e}^{-x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)={e}^{-1}$ to find $K:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-1} & =K{e}^{-{e}^{-0}} \\\\ {e}^{-1} & =K{e}^{-1} \\\\ K & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y{e}^{-x}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)={e}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-{e}^{-x}}.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275111 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274760 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275111 1069 "q-14313" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275111 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275111 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}$  that satisfies $y(1)=2.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275111 1072 #uuid "c2372277-7745-54d3-839c-0b3553acea76" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275111 1074 "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\int {y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {y}^{3} & ={x}^{3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere   $C=3{C}_{1}$ is an arbitrary constant.  \n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{3} & ={1}^{3}+C \\\\ 8-1 & =C \\\\ C & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}={x}^{3}+7.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274680 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275112 1069 "q-14314" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275112 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275112 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}})+\\frac{3y}{x}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275112 1072 #uuid "b4e19450-4e51-548d-b019-a1f8398b116f" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275112 1074 "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}})+\\frac{3y}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{v}^{2}{x}^{2}})+\\frac{3vx}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}})+3v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}})+2v \\\\ (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}})+2v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275113 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274886 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275113 1069 "q-14316" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275113 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275113 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275113 1072 #uuid "f48980a9-f2e7-5b29-aaff-6351dbf19d07" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275113 1074 "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-x(vx)+(vx)^{2}}{(vx)^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{2}(1-v+{v}^{2})}{{x}^{2}({v}^{2}+1)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{1-v+{v}^{2}}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1-v+{v}^{2}}{{v}^{2}+1}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1-v+{v}^{2}-{v}^{3}-v}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1-2v+{v}^{2}-{v}^{3}}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{1-2v+{v}^{2}-{v}^{3}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275114 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274835 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275114 1069 "q-14318" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275114 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275114 1071 "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{yx}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275114 1072 #uuid "c139f739-608f-51ea-bcb4-01f8a92a8c21" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275114 1074 "With $y=v(x)x$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{yx}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{(vx)x}{(vx)^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{v{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}({v}^{2}+1)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v}{{v}^{2}+1}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v-{v}^{3}-v}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-{v}^{3}}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{{v}^{3}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{{v}^{3}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (\\frac{1}{v}+\\frac{1}{{v}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}v & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|v|-\\frac{1}{2{v}^{2}} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{C}_{1} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v|-\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2{C}_{1} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v|-\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we introduced $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our solution, we finally obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}|-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}} & =C \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}} & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275115 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274828 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275115 1069 "q-14319" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275115 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275115 1071 "Which of the following is a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275115 1072 #uuid "fc348cb6-a91b-542b-84f6-f5b771ae4157" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275115 1074 "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+{x}^{3}}.$\n\nWe see that both the numerator and denominator are homogeneous polynomials of degree $3.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $3-3=0.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+{x}^{3}}\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)^{3}-3(tx)^{2}(ty)+(tx)(ty)^{2}}{(ty)^{3}+(tx)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{3}({x}^{3})-{t}^{3}(3{x}^{2}y)+{t}^{3}(x{y}^{2})}{{t}^{3}{y}^{3}+{t}^{3}{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{3}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})}{{t}^{3}({y}^{3}+{x}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is homogeneous." 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275116 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274733 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275116 1069 "q-14321" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275116 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275116 1071 "Find the general solution of the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{3}+x{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}y+{x}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}y}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275116 1072 #uuid "30f68eaf-916e-557d-ad03-5e30a2efe0b7" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275116 1074 "This is a homogeneous equation because it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y),\n$$\n\nand we notice that all terms in the numerator and denominator of $f(x,y)$ are of order $3$ (i.e., they are cubic).\n\nUsing the transformation $y=v(x)x$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{3}+x{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}y+{x}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}y} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{(vx)^{3}+x(vx)^{2}+{x}^{2}(vx)+{x}^{3}}{x(vx)^{2}+{x}^{2}(vx)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{3}({v}^{3}+{v}^{2}+v+1)}{{x}^{3}({v}^{2}+v)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{v}^{3}+{v}^{2}+v+1}{{v}^{2}+v} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{3}+{v}^{2}+v+1}{{v}^{2}+v}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{3}+{v}^{2}+v+1-{v}^{3}-{v}^{2}}{{v}^{2}+v} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v+1}{v(v+1)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{v} \\\\ v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int v\\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we introduced $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above into our solution, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275117 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274932 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275117 1069 "q-14322" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275117 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275117 1071 "Which of the following is NOT a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275117 1072 #uuid "a9372b90-e355-5e25-8427-4c0e772f932c" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275117 1074 "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is NOT the case is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{y}^{2}+xy}\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{y}^{2}+xy}.$\n\nWe see that the numerator is NOT a homogeneous polynomial. Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is NOT a homogeneous function.\n\nHence, the original differential equation is  NOT homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{y}^{2}+xy}\\,\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is not homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\nFor $x=1$, $y=2$ and $t=3$, for instance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =f(2\\cdot 1,2\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =f(2,6) \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}+1}{{6}^{2}+2(6)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{48}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =f(1,2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{2}+1}{{2}^{2}+1(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, there exists at least one combination of values of $x$, $y$ and $t$ for which the identity $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y)$ is not observed.\n\nHence, the original differential equation is  NOT homogeneous." 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275118 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274973 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275118 1069 "q-14323" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275118 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275118 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+2y{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275118 1072 #uuid "590e41cc-f7e5-52a9-8e4b-08ef663bf420" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275118 1074 "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+2y{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{3}+x(vx)^{2}-(vx)^{3}}{x(vx)^{2}+2(vx){x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{3}(1+{v}^{2}-{v}^{3})}{{x}^{3}({v}^{2}+2v)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{1+{v}^{2}-{v}^{3}}{{v}^{2}+2v} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1+{v}^{2}-{v}^{3}}{{v}^{2}+2v}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1+{v}^{2}-{v}^{3}-{v}^{3}-2{v}^{2}}{{v}^{2}+2v} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1-{v}^{2}-2{v}^{3}}{{v}^{2}+2v} \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}+2v}{1-{v}^{2}-2{v}^{3}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275119 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274756 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275119 1069 "q-143317" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275119 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275119 1071 "The marginal mass functions of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(1,1).$\n\n| | $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ | |  | | $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.5$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.35$ | |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |  |  |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ |  |  |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.5$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.35$ |" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275119 1072 #uuid "92d98773-4cd0-585a-813d-5cb9eff8