ot (20) \\\\  & =700.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274681 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275236 1069 "q-149683" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275236 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275236 1071 "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275236 1072 #uuid "22545d4e-216d-56de-bbb1-cb8300d938d2" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275236 1074 "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=6,\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275237 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274728 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275237 1069 "q-149684" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275237 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275237 1071 "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275237 1072 #uuid "653984cb-dc72-5c76-a905-1afdf47beb69" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275237 1074 "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274670 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275238 1069 "q-149685" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275238 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275238 1071 "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275238 1072 #uuid "670ec23b-ca96-5b00-8ddc-e1d0726992ef" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275238 1074 "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275239 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274916 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275239 1069 "q-149688" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275239 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275239 1071 "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275239 1072 #uuid "d820f8e1-5858-5b82-b3dd-c312fda5c92f" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275239 1074 "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274667 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275240 1069 "q-149689" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275240 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275240 1071 "Find the value of the double sum\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{10}{a}_{i}({b}_{j}-5)\n$$\n\ngiven that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{a}_{i}=25,\\,{\\sum }_{j=1}^{10}{b}_{j}=58.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275240 1072 #uuid "f4fc0237-5fbe-5917-b09d-ca598cb6acd1" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275240 1074 "The product rule for double summations states that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{i}{b}_{j}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{a}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{b}_{j}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the product rule for double summations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{10}{a}_{i}({b}_{j}-5) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{a}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{10}({b}_{j}-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the rules for single summations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{a}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{10}({b}_{j}-5) & =({\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{a}_{i})\\cdot ({\\sum }_{j=1}^{10}{b}_{j}-{\\sum }_{j=1}^{10}5) \\\\  & =({\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{a}_{i})\\cdot ({\\sum }_{j=1}^{10}{b}_{j}-5{\\sum }_{j=1}^{10}1) \\\\  & =(25)\\cdot (58-5\\cdot 10) \\\\  & =(25)\\cdot (58-50) \\\\  & =(25)\\cdot (8) \\\\  & =200\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275241 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275047 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275241 1069 "q-149710" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275241 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275241 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt[3]{x-2y}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x-2y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275241 1072 #uuid "dabceef6-e8d3-5182-b240-ffcea0ff3af5" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275241 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x-2y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1-2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\sqrt[3]{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1-2\\sqrt[3]{z}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=1-2\\sqrt[3]{z}.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274944 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275242 1069 "q-149714" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275242 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275242 1071 "![](images/28/2860103dc3d1019cddcffa504b3cce1fb8a84cfd046bc796712d1e16516d5269.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $\\frac{x}{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(2,e),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275242 1072 #uuid "ba25d72c-66e5-5660-8cd6-858347d6e9a3" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275242 1074 "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,⟹\\,x=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le e\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le e$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2}{t})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=4y{e}^{2x}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4y{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =4t{e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t} \\\\  & =4t({t}^{4}) \\\\  & =4{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(4{t}^{5})\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274980 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275243 1069 "q-149717" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275243 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275243 1071 "Find the value of the double sum\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{20}({a}_{i}+1)({b}_{j}-1)\n$$\n\ngiven that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{a}_{i}=20,\\,{\\sum }_{j=1}^{20}{b}_{j}=26.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275243 1072 #uuid "c995cbf9-c89f-5f4e-b314-1e2fc502c516" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275243 1074 "The product rule for double summations states that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{i}{b}_{j}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{a}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{b}_{j}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the product rule for double summations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{20}({a}_{i}+1)({b}_{j}-1) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}({a}_{i}+1){\\sum }_{j=1}^{20}({b}_{j}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the rules for single summations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}({a}_{i}+1){\\sum }_{j=1}^{20}({b}_{j}-1) & =({\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{a}_{i}+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1)\\cdot ({\\sum }_{j=1}^{20}{b}_{j}-{\\sum }_{j=1}^{20}1) \\\\  & =(20+5)\\cdot (26-20) \\\\  & =(25)\\cdot (6) \\\\  & =150.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275244 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275016 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275244 1069 "q-149721" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275244 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275244 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3-{e}^{y-3x}.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275244 1072 #uuid "e2be2a98-4693-5e20-8a16-811d290d52b9" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275244 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=y-3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+3 & =3-{e}^{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-z}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ -\\int {e}^{-z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{-z} & =x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n{e}^{3x-y}=x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)={e}^{3x-y}.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274920 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275245 1069 "q-149726" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275245 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275245 1071 "Find the value of the double sum\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{20}5{b}_{j}({a}_{i}-4)\n$$\n\ngiven that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{a}_{i}=85,\\,{\\sum }_{j=1}^{20}{b}_{j}=18.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275245 1072 #uuid "e2588658-5d1a-5a0f-8531-44be192b2584" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275245 1074 "The product rule for double summations states that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{i}{b}_{j}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{a}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{b}_{j}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the product rule for double summations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{20}5{b}_{j}({a}_{i}-4) & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{20}5{b}_{j}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}({a}_{i}-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the rules for single summations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{20}5{b}_{j}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}({a}_{i}-4) & =(5{\\sum }_{j=1}^{20}{b}_{j})\\cdot ({\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{a}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}4) \\\\  & =(5{\\sum }_{j=1}^{20}{b}_{j})\\cdot ({\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{a}_{i}-4{\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}1) \\\\  & =(5\\cdot 18)\\cdot (85-4\\cdot 20) \\\\  & =(90)\\cdot (85-80) \\\\  & =(90)\\cdot (5) \\\\  & =450\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275246 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274827 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275246 1069 "q-149727" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275246 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275246 1071 "![](images/8d/8d060e257d74e6e0e6b7f35bfb89e9e974499059ddf4d029c0cace195ba67c13.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y-x}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $x=2y-4$ from $(0,2)$ to $(2,3),$ as shown above." 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275246 1072 #uuid "9463919f-5148-546d-ad7d-95a446b32afe" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275246 1074 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2t-4.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2t-4,\\,t⟩,\\,2\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $2\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-4)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-x},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{t-(2t-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y-x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=4-t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{1}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{1}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-0] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274748 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275247 1069 "q-149736" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275247 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275247 1071 "Find the value of the double sum\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{15}({a}_{i}-2)({b}_{j}+3)\n$$\n\ngiven that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{a}_{i}=35,\\,{\\sum }_{j=1}^{15}{b}_{j}=20.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275247 1072 #uuid "bfc32615-aaad-527a-9e69-bb95159fcc66" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275247 1074 "The product rule for double summations states that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{i}{b}_{j}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{a}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{b}_{j}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the product rule for double summations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{15}({a}_{i}-2)({b}_{j}+3) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}({a}_{i}-2){\\sum }_{j=1}^{15}({b}_{j}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the rules for single summations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}({a}_{i}-2){\\sum }_{j=1}^{15}({b}_{j}+3) & =({\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{a}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}2)\\cdot ({\\sum }_{j=1}^{15}{b}_{j}+{\\sum }_{j=1}^{15}3) \\\\  & =({\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{a}_{i}-2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}1)\\cdot ({\\sum }_{j=1}^{15}{b}_{j}+3{\\sum }_{j=1}^{15}1) \\\\  & =(35-2\\cdot 15)\\cdot (20+3\\cdot 15) \\\\  & =(35-30)\\cdot (20+45) \\\\  & =(5)\\cdot (65) \\\\  & =325.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274962 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275248 1069 "q-149742" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275248 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275248 1071 "Find the value of the double sum\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{11}{a}_{i}({b}_{j}-6)\n$$\n\ngiven that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{a}_{i}=15,\\,{\\sum }_{j=1}^{11}{b}_{j}=68.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275248 1072 #uuid "23573361-6825-5dbd-b75f-5bad3a52d8aa" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275248 1074 "The product rule for double summations states that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{i}{b}_{j}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{a}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{b}_{j}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the product rule for double summations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{11}{a}_{i}({b}_{j}-6) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{a}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{11}({b}_{j}-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the rules for single summations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{a}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{11}({b}_{j}-6) & =({\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{a}_{i})\\cdot ({\\sum }_{j=1}^{11}{b}_{j}-{\\sum }_{j=1}^{11}6) \\\\  & =({\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{a}_{i})\\cdot ({\\sum }_{j=1}^{11}{b}_{j}-6{\\sum }_{j=1}^{11}1) \\\\  & =(15)\\cdot (68-6\\cdot 11) \\\\  & =(15)\\cdot (68-66) \\\\  & =(15)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275249 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274972 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275249 1069 "q-149744" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275249 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275249 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{10x+2y}{5x+y+1}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=5x+y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275249 1072 #uuid "5fd5b90f-0ac6-584e-ad1f-769d139ad924" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275249 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2(5x+y)}{(5x+y)+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=5x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=5+\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-5 & =\\frac{2z}{z+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{7z+5}{z+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=\\frac{7z+5}{z+1}.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275250 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274982 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275250 1069 "q-149745" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275250 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275250 1071 "Evaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x={e}^{y/2}$ from $(1,0)$ to $(\\sqrt{2},\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275250 1072 #uuid "b09217dd-ddfe-59be-95cc-af28d1d6d361" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275250 1074 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)={e}^{t/2}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{e}^{t/2},\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t/2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{2}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{2})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{e}^{t}}{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y} \\\\  & =6{({e}^{t/2})}^{6}{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =6{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}6{e}^{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={e}^{t}+4,$ $\\text{d}u={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t & =3{\\int }_{5}^{6}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{5}^{6} \\\\  & =2({6}^{3/2}-{5}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =2(6\\sqrt{6}-5\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{6}-10\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275251 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274905 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275251 1069 "q-149748" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275251 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275251 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x-2, & x\\le 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{x}, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=4$\n- $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=4$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275251 1072 #uuid "c0a8af66-eea1-5639-ab77-2bc1523780a5" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275251 1074 "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $f(x)$ at $x=4.$\n\nLet's start by computing $f(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=4,$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x) & =4-2 \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & =f(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=4,$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x) & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & =f(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=4,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275252 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275078 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275252 1069 "q-149751" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275252 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275252 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $h(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\nh(x)={\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{-x}, & x\\le -1 \\\\ 1-2x, & x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=-1$\n- $h(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-1$\n- $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275252 1072 #uuid "a65475e2-5e0e-5ab6-8415-f174e9dcfcbc" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275252 1074 "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $h(x)$ at $x=-1.$\n\nLet's start by computing $h(-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-1) & =-\\sqrt{-(-1)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=-1$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}h(x) & =-\\sqrt{-(-1)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & =h(-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is *not* right-continuous at $x=-1$ since the right-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}h(x) & =1-2(-1) \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3 \\\\  & \\ne h(-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274890 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275253 1069 "q-149756" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275253 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275253 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\sqrt{4x-4y}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275253 1072 #uuid "282836bd-3e6d-5985-8015-1e73c28407a8" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275253 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1+2\\sqrt{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1+2\\sqrt{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2\\sqrt{z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ -\\int \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ -\\sqrt{z} & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\sqrt{x-y}=x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=-\\sqrt{x-y}.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274764 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275254 1069 "q-149815" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275254 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275254 1071 "![](images/62/624bb949d80c1da50cba951b00210a2a26aeb3c44d77d4c2eed69c8e3ba0f4d4.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},$ shown above." 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275254 1072 #uuid "4554a25e-9962-5d01-9655-91afa5f0b772" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275254 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275255 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275002 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275255 1069 "q-149817" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275255 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275255 1071 "![](images/b2/b2ce73d5a44c834b5935596e85293eb92039b674746754317846488dbba8d930.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={e}^{\\theta /4}\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,$ shown above." 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275255 1072 #uuid "a9e01f54-95c4-5242-b6c8-c41e6563c805" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275255 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={e}^{\\theta /4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{\\theta /4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{{({e}^{\\theta /4})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{\\theta /4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{{e}^{\\theta /2}+\\frac{1}{16}\\,{e}^{\\theta /2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{16}\\,{e}^{\\theta /2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{e}^{\\theta /4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}\\,{e}^{\\theta /4}\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}({e}^{\\pi /2}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}({e}^{\\pi /2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275256 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275068 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275256 1069 "q-149818" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275256 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275256 1071 "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $AB=BA={I}_{4}$\n- $AC=CA={I}_{4}$\n- $C={A}^{-1}$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275256 1072 #uuid "b4a87f3f-1f71-5f82-bc66-df6d75d97be5" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275256 1074 "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{4}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0