2186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275258 1071 "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $AB=BA={I}_{3}$\n- $AC=CA={I}_{3}$\n- $B={A}^{-1}$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275258 1072 #uuid "bdbd7939-21af-5d98-9fc2-2a05d548f266" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275258 1074 "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{3}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{3}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false. As a result, $B\\ne {A}^{-1}$ and statement III is false too.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{3}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $CA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CA & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274763 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275259 1069 "q-149833" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275259 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275259 1071 "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nE=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,F=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,G=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $EF=FE={I}_{3}$\n- $FG=GF={I}_{3}$\n- $F={G}^{-1}$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275259 1072 #uuid "1e4c02d8-0c31-5630-b78b-ca07d9d8a348" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275259 1074 "- First, we check whether $EF=FE={I}_{3}.$ Computing $EF$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}EF & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $FE$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}FE & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n- Now, we check whether $FG=GF={I}_{3}.$ Computing $FG$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}FG & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-8 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{3}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false. As a result, $F\\ne {G}^{-1}$ and statement III is false too.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275260 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275061 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275260 1069 "q-149909" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275260 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275260 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for the polar curve $r=-\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275260 1072 #uuid "7434f2a6-72f3-5e31-9dcb-4b1f63dafdbd" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275260 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ and $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }.$ \n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nas parametric equations of a curve in terms of $\\theta .$ \n\nUsing the fact that $r=-\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\nx=-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nThen, to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275261 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274815 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275261 1069 "q-149913" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275261 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275261 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for the polar curve $r=-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275261 1072 #uuid "5af8ac95-f846-5704-b546-5b84be0ef466" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275261 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).$ Also, since $r=-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dx}{d\\theta } & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ))}{-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}{-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274766 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275262 1069 "q-150011" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275262 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275262 1071 "![](images/0d/0d2aa60719643235f5ae684d59607199731626e15bac40fd6b14d071c099ab4a.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275262 1072 #uuid "08ceb92c-03d7-50b0-a12d-3ba81bdd7b99" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275262 1074 "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/2b/2b8ef08638551571caa43150080a8550a0b61ed24eaa7c6da73fe66edd161e8e.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/28/28db5c349d3455c58f81da7c181586ba4e0ad5662f35faa2575d6622b3713988.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274899 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275263 1069 "q-150014" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275263 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275263 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x+1)$ at the point $(0,0).$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275263 1072 #uuid "2b8ebe67-7d90-5f2a-b18d-1cdea332312f" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275263 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{ln}⁡u,\\,u=3x+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3x+1}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={\\frac{3}{3x+1}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3(0)+1} \\\\  & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(0,0)$ is $m=3.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =3\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275264 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274929 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275264 1069 "q-150015" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275264 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275264 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $f(x)=ln⁡(2-x)$ at the point $(1,0).$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275264 1072 #uuid "329ee6a2-6455-51c9-9cfd-81f75012b6f0" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275264 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=ln⁡u,\\,u=2-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-x}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={\\frac{1}{x-2}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1)-2} \\\\  & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(1,0)$ is $m=-1.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =(-1)\\cdot (x-1) \\\\ y & =-x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275265 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275090 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275265 1069 "q-150018" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275265 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275265 1071 "![](images/67/678ab825415e107bca9554d90e2ad5a4d8cc90a5ef68d817972ef98dfc165922.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n- ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275265 1072 #uuid "5a5b0685-d096-5d41-9e82-0f5600340c35" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275265 1074 "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/0e/0e8d0fcf619987f49869cc6ae9eb5cb0c7acbbb23f45ce8f64626dfd1debdc63.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/b2/b2bec1f1da172b623f20d28fd0e9c5a248cf00dc621769851ca33b3b545b8592.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275266 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274976 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275266 1069 "q-15122" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275266 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275266 1071 "Which of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275266 1072 #uuid "8ac3b5b3-7e20-5bd6-a6af-097e3f624a37" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275266 1074 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ))^{2}+(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )+36{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )+9{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta ))}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )+9{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274978 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275267 1069 "q-15129" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275267 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275267 1071 "![](images/59/59214bcbfd4b1d718faa0c2355e932ab3cedae5b785b17497cef554ee3ec099b.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=3-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275267 1072 #uuid "e06591c4-f9ec-5725-a82d-146c8f26267d" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275267 1074 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(3-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ))^{2}+(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )+9{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+1+8{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{10-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+8{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274645 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275268 1069 "q-15130" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275268 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275268 1071 "The equation of a curve is ${x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}=6.$ The normal line at the point $(-3,\\,1)$ on the curve intersects the $x$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275268 1072 #uuid "ff4af0d3-ebf7-53d5-b132-2755c7e5429b" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275268 1074 "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(-3,\\,1).$  Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{′},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2x-6y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ -6y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-2x}{-6y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x}{3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(-3,\\,1)$ is \n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(-3,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{x}{3y})}_{(-3,\\,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{3\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is ${m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{-1}=1.$ We can write the equation of the normal at the point $(-3,\\,1)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-1) & =1(x+3) \\\\ y & =(x+3)+1 \\\\ y & =x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $x$-axis. At the point $P$ the $y$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $y=0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+4 \\\\ 0 & =x+4 \\\\ x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(-4,\\,0).$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275269 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274965 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275269 1069 "q-15131" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275269 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275269 1071 "![](images/00/00e5e0f5c47a056beaca2d7bc1b1d10a6a11f96398df7400e69c023f5780b8a6.png)\n\nWhich of the following integrals gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )$ for $-\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}$ (shown in blue in the  diagram above)?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275269 1072 #uuid "915da383-5cd0-54bd-ae8a-9f0bc95c9cf2" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275269 1074 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )+4{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+1+3{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+3{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274685 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275270 1069 "q-15163" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275270 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275270 1071 "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,y=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\nfrom $t=2$ to $t=5$ ?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275270 1072 #uuid "b418c2bf-fd1a-57e4-845f-5c5fb4e1bb93" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275270 1074 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))=1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{1+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+4{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275271 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274643 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275271 1069 "q-152045" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275271 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275271 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(2x+4)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275271 1072 #uuid "9866e046-eb23-5536-9118-9338cb3fa801" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275271 1074 "First, we substitute $u=2x+4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2x+4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $4$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(2x+4)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}(\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(2x+4)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{4}^{a}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-2{u}^{-1/2}]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275272 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275080 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275272 1069 "q-152056" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275272 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275272 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}+1)^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275272 1072 #uuid "8b4ebe1c-7aff-5381-8ad5-281043ebfb60" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275272 1074 "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}+1)^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-5/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-5/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{-3/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{a}^{3}}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275273 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274807 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275273 1069 "q-152073" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275273 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275273 1071 "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275273 1072 #uuid "d17f43cd-3cd5-50eb-b926-556ea9fc28cf" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275273 1074 "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{x}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-2)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275274 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274754 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275274 1069 "q-152077" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275274 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275274 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{6}{(1+2x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275274 1072 #uuid "2e55de38-da94-5dd6-b923-292738e6c28b" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275274 1074 "First, we substitute $u=1+2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=1+2x$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6}{(1+2x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{6}{{u}^{3}}(\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{6}{(1+2x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-2}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-1)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274865 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275275 1069 "q-152080" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275275 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275275 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{3}+2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275275 1072 #uuid "689b5589-3654-5d50-a22a-379b12d716b1" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275275 1074 "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}+2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}+2$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-25$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{3}+2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-25}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{3}+2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-25}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u. \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-25}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-25} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-25} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-25)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{1}{25}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{75}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275276 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275065 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275276 1069 "q-152081" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275276 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275276 1071 "${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275276 1072 #uuid "1c663ba0-cdf4-5441-8958-07612047feb8" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275276 1074 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)]_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)] \\\\  & =10\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/27/27ec65761e5119be666e69b67b2fa2fcb7b4f3665ddc41faddd6c136d5c955b1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275277 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274662 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275277 1069 "q-152083" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275277 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275277 1071 "${\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275277 1072 #uuid "c335a586-4972-57a6-aeb8-51c204a11abc" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275277 1074 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(z)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(z)]_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})] \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})] \\\\  & =4\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/27/27ec65761e5119be666e69b67b2fa2fcb7b4f3665ddc41faddd6c136d5c955b1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =4\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275278 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274782 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275278 1069 "q-152090" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275278 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275278 1071 "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{8}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275278 1072 #uuid "53acf6af-1ba0-53f8-a74a-497c15b89d6f" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275278 1074 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(z)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{8}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{1}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(z)]_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)] \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)])\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/27/27ec65761e5119be666e69b67b2fa2fcb7b4f3665ddc41faddd6c136d5c955b1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{8}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275279 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274933 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275279 1069 "q-152121" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275279 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275279 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})}\\frac{6}{1+36{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275279 1072 #uuid "e1492e1a-9218-5fa3-bf2f-e2545568402e" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275279 1074 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})}\\frac{6}{1+36{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})}\\frac{1}{1+36{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})}\\frac{1}{1+(6x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x)]}_{a}^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6a)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6a)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/fa/faa31c8261818c5c978d984a9b90a13e58794b02ff6f47e868ad6d5a06b7f71a.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})}\\frac{6}{1+36{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6a)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275280 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275010 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275280 1069 "q-152124" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275280 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275280 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{6}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275280 1072 #uuid "b7650cdf-2252-5e38-9fb6-8aa191ffc73a" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275280 1074 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{6}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{1}{1+(2x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)]}_{a}^{-\\sqrt{3}/2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)] \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)) \\\\  & =-\\pi -3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/38/382ca84f05af04af9c9b2c837d549a09b695574781f12bc67b8438b95b5f5d75.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\pi -3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)] \\\\  & =-\\pi -3\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\pi +\\frac{3\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275281 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274792 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275281 1069 "q-152128" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275281 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275281 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275281 1072 #uuid "59bc79b0-5c78-54d5-a3cd-02ce7009facd" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275281 1074 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{k})+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})]-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/04/042ad2450306552d9d5a7e4219d15547ad7104601b7ce6350a64193981de0de7.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})]-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275282 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274692 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275282 1069 "q-152132" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275282 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275282 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275282 1072 #uuid "64c1c666-99ef-5c2c-a646-cdbcd9708079" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275282 1074 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{k})+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{5}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{5}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+{5}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{z}{5})]}_{5}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{5})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{5})-\\frac{\\pi }{4}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5}),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/f8/f8325b1ea152bd40aaa909202bfce59e621952486041c657c0e27b9192ecec03.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{5})-\\frac{\\pi }{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275283 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274706 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275283 1069 "q-15221" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275283 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275283 1071 "At an archaeological site, an object is found with $30%$ of its original carbon-14 content. Given that the half-life of carbon-14 is $5\\,730$ years, approximately how old is the object?" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275283 1072 #uuid "1467861e-7fb4-522f-8278-a2b816199885" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275283 1074 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=5\\,730$ years, we have\n\n$$\n5\\,730=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5\\,730}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.3{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.3{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.3{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730} \\\\ 0.3\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730} \\\\ 0.3 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.3) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.3) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5\\,730} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{5\\,730\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.3)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈9\\,953\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the object is approximately $9\\,953$ years old." 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275284 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274878 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275284 1069 "q-152230" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275284 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275284 1071 "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275284 1072 #uuid "99ecb2ab-6fc0-5126-a4bf-6487dcd087bc" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275284 1074 "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({2}^{1}-{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (2-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{2}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/b9/b9a29cbd8a029e0f26e3da6a042ada0f8a809053233979bb4ff73db3dcf0f10d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (2-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275085 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275285 1069 "q-152232" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275285 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275285 1071 "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{2}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275285 1072 #uuid "5442b57e-dc80-591b-9658-b81dc5e1f54d" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275285 1074 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{2}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{3x}}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{3x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{2}^{3x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/bd/bdf8e7dad9550977ad6bd207b43cd75e91226ed84c2807dac3d023470d9e02ce.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{2}^{3x} & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274770 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275286 1069 "q-15225" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275286 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275286 1071 "![](images/6c/6c70d5f15424c4d7d67a3998a785a214e46e8dfa47b83839fd99a574b2f756cf.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x)$, which is defined on $[-1,6]$, is shown above. It consists of three line segments and the arc of the semi-circle centered at $(4,0)$ with radius $1.$ Find the average value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5].$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275286 1072 #uuid "9f59744c-aeb0-5310-8ef4-47c19ef23828" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275286 1074 "The average value of a function on a given interval $[a,b]$ is \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the additive property of the definite integral, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe 1st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,3].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe 2nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,5].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula for the average value, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5-0}\\cdot (3+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274646 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275287 1069 "q-152255" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275287 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275287 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275287 1072 #uuid "ffe27b4d-cc2f-5257-bb59-c4fcd50f69da" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275287 1074 "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{3}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{3}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](images/ff/ff41c6fa5858f45e76a4d4fec0f0a8080300b69fcb632df60141d93c66f3cdd9.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275071 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275288 1069 "q-152257" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275288 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275288 1071 "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{1-x}}}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275288 1072 #uuid "acc8670d-da21-5f34-929c-0033eaf8bf44" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275288 1074 "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{1-x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{1-x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{1-x}}}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{\\infty }^{1}{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{1-x}}}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[-{e}^{-u}]_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{{e}^{a}}+\\frac{1}{e}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{a}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](images/4e/4ef3194e946aec805866202a5e0ad521291d71b1fabc2df68d6cb1a4d93da42b.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{1-x}}}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{e}-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{a}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274968 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275289 1069 "q-152260" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275289 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275289 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275289 1072 #uuid "7923289d-2b9c-5e4c-879e-b391c8aa2edb" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275289 1074 "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\