 are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274987 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275405 1069 "q-157476" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275405 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275405 1071 "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $x-2y+2z=3$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Π$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275405 1072 #uuid "99eda82b-48bc-5827-9c44-f2725d46a5d4" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275405 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3+2y-2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+2z & =3 \\\\ (0)-2(0)+2(0) & =3 \\\\ 0 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider $a=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3+1 \\\\ 0+0 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+2z & =3 \\\\ (4)-2(0)+2(1) & =3 \\\\ 6 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275406 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274651 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275406 1069 "q-157493" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275406 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275406 1071 "The vector-valued function $r(x,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [-1,1].$ Which of the following could be $r?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275406 1072 #uuid "e9ccb25e-fe91-5f21-a4c1-a069fbca392a" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275406 1074 "![](images/56/56a380b075d10862170ed9c9a45c07dddff3eaef1ea9fb3109a61cc3d27b5ac4.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [a,b]$ has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(x)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [-1,1].$ \n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\nr(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [-1,1],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275407 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274872 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275407 1069 "q-15759" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275407 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275407 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275407 1072 #uuid "051f821d-0a77-5a88-85da-69a3ef815c14" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275407 1074 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=x \\\\ y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡t & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =\\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used here that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t>0$ in the range $0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, using the definition of a tangent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡t}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=x$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275408 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274913 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275408 1069 "q-15761" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275408 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275408 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t,\\,t\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275408 1072 #uuid "3486395c-c23b-50d0-ac86-019f33fdad15" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275408 1074 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$ by ${sin}^{2}⁡t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\ 1+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} \\\\ 1+{cot}^{2}⁡t & ={csc}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{x}{3}$ and $\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${y}^{2}=1+{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275409 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274727 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275409 1069 "q-15792" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275409 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275409 1071 "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275409 1072 #uuid "3ccbcc93-cb2d-5c7d-ae5c-a7e728fdad2b" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275409 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275410 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275083 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275410 1069 "q-15793" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275410 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275410 1071 "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275410 1072 #uuid "2463b954-08db-52f2-9a44-620e52671ff5" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275410 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =4(1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275411 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274679 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275411 1069 "q-158009" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275411 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275411 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation ${x}^{2}\\,{y}^{′}-y=3$ for $y\\ne -3.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275411 1072 #uuid "e0c7db14-c9c5-588a-b927-09334c1e0842" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275411 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\,{y}^{′}-y & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y+3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y+3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y+3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y+3}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y+3| & =-\\frac{1}{x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y+3|=-\\frac{1}{x}+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y+3| & =-\\frac{1}{x}+C \\\\ |y+3| & ={e}^{-1/x+C} \\\\ |y+3| & ={e}^{-1/x}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ |y+3| & =K{e}^{-1/x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}$ and $K>0.$\n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|y+3| & =K{e}^{-1/x} \\\\ y+3 & =K{e}^{-1/x} \\\\ y & =K{e}^{-1/x}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275412 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274672 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275412 1069 "q-158010" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275412 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275412 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\,{y}^{′}-y=0$ for $y\\ne 0$ and $|x|<1.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275412 1072 #uuid "6f88ec76-892c-5986-98b6-3866ba9a8457" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275412 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\,{y}^{′}-y & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ |y| & =K{e}^{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}$ and $K>0.$\n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =K{e}^{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275413 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274767 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275413 1069 "q-158012" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275413 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275413 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x}{3{y}^{2}}.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275413 1072 #uuid "088db977-5f34-5d61-a165-97cdc5e6df38" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275413 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x}{3{y}^{2}} \\\\ 3{y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 3{y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {y}^{3} & ={x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}={x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+C}.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275414 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275089 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275414 1069 "q-158013" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275414 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275414 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}$ for $y\\ne 0$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275414 1072 #uuid "253f761f-d9dd-516e-a877-3691e4e75b8a" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275414 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{y}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ |y| & =K{e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}$ and $K>0.$\n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\ny=K{e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275415 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274738 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275415 1069 "q-158014" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275415 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275415 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $y\\ne 0.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275415 1072 #uuid "2c6ab8c9-fca1-56b1-82a8-98c4fa9f8c2c" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [1