\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275425 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275041 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275425 1069 "q-158027" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275425 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275425 1071 "Separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275425 1072 #uuid "c0d607e3-0c1a-5227-a757-1fdd9227cb73" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275425 1074 "A differentiable equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differentiable equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot {y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275426 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274693 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275426 1069 "q-158029" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275426 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275426 1071 "![](images/73/7359571270dd1ce7df2b68d396e2c87cbf44bd7b18adf94bdb7ade4c02a8bc12.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275426 1072 #uuid "4ecd1a30-d61f-5d31-96d2-62fa52545c5c" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275426 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a set that does not contain the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a set that does not contain the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace.  In this case, we have that the vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None\"." 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275427 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274775 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275427 1069 "q-158030" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275427 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275427 1071 "Separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{y}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275427 1072 #uuid "7037401f-27c0-588d-a952-7eb3ef7b14ef" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275427 1074 "A differentiable equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differentiable equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\ny\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275428 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275042 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275428 1069 "q-158032" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275428 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275428 1071 "Separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3x}{5xy+y}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275428 1072 #uuid "fe104c00-01e9-59b5-87fa-d82fed8f3827" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275428 1074 "A differentiable equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differentiable equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3x}{(5x+1)y} \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{5x+1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\ny\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3x}{5x+1}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275429 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274702 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275429 1069 "q-158035" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275429 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275429 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275429 1072 #uuid "448a7a77-8f84-5ac7-87e1-3803e2799bbe" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275429 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡y & ={x}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡y={x}^{3}+C.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274954 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275430 1069 "q-158038" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275430 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275430 1071 "If $tan⁡x=3,$ then $tan⁡(-x)=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275430 1072 #uuid "e8cb683f-c83e-5880-b481-a77cc9d224dc" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275430 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275431 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274780 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275431 1069 "q-158041" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275431 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275431 1071 "If $tan⁡x=-4,$ then $\\frac{1}{2}tan⁡(-x+3\\pi )+3=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275431 1072 #uuid "6710fdcd-157b-570c-95e4-ed16e27fba49" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275431 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x+3\\pi ) & =tan⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-(-4) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}tan⁡(-x+3\\pi )+3 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2}+3 \\\\  & =2+3 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275432 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274721 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275432 1069 "q-158044" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275432 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275432 1071 "If $sec⁡x=\\sqrt{6},$ then $sec⁡(-x)=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275432 1072 #uuid "0b84288a-81b8-54b0-94c6-6bddb05e4bd4" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275432 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](images/fb/fbf6ef1ca91f998ab4dc2dda1890ffc8a713f2eeff19e5569351b2466256d63a.png)\n\nNotice that our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. If we reflect the graph about the $y$-axis, we get the same picture. This means that $sec⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡(-x)=sec⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡(-x) & =sec⁡x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275433 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275038 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275433 1069 "q-158046" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275433 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275433 1071 "If $tan⁡x=-2,$ then $\\frac{3}{5}tan⁡(-x-7\\pi )+\\frac{4}{5}=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275433 1072 #uuid "e6dd4ab2-db9a-5355-9ce0-ec6183a9429b" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275433 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x-7\\pi ) & =tan⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-(-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}tan⁡(-x-7\\pi )+\\frac{4}{5} & =\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot 2+\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275434 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274816 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275434 1069 "q-158047" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275434 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275434 1071 "If $cos⁡a=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},$ then $cos⁡(-a)=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275434 1072 #uuid "12661d5b-e4d5-5ee9-8284-57ed504ec5ab" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275434 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/23/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nNotice that our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. If we reflect the graph over the $y$-axis, we get the same picture. This means that $cos⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡(-x)=cos⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-a) & =cos⁡a \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275435 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274713 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275435 1069 "q-158049" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275435 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275435 1071 "If $\\operatorname{sec}⁡a=\\sqrt{7},$ then $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-a+20\\pi )=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275435 1072 #uuid "dc90373f-5a39-5101-be93-517f135c4a77" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275435 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/fb/fbf6ef1ca91f998ab4dc2dda1890ffc8a713f2eeff19e5569351b2466256d63a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-a+20\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-a+2\\pi \\cdot 10) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-a) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡a \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-a+20\\pi )=\\sqrt{7}.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275436 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274969 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275436 1069 "q-158052" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275436 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275436 1071 "If $cos⁡x=-1,$ then $cos⁡(-x+24\\pi )=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275436 1072 #uuid "0e0baa0c-c163-5abd-bc09-5322350948ee" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275436 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/23/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea