$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-x+8\\pi )+7 & =6(-\\frac{5}{3})+7 \\\\  & =-10+7 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275438 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275021 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275438 1069 "q-158055" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275438 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275438 1071 "If $cot⁡x=\\frac{5}{2},$ then $cot⁡(-x)=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275438 1072 #uuid "0221b117-6ad9-5a2f-80bb-79e143dc5ca6" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275438 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cot⁡x.$\n\n![](images/6e/6eb672fe024f024f20c7c3c80947d1b47bade8f998e705d6f411ae3f298ecde2.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $cot⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡(-x)=-cot⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡(-x) & =-cot⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275439 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275028 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275439 1069 "q-158058" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275439 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275439 1071 "If $cot⁡a=-7,$ then $cot⁡(-a+5\\pi )=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275439 1072 #uuid "1d482a12-cb86-542a-9d10-1273500fd3d0" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275439 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cot⁡x.$\n\n![](images/6e/6eb672fe024f024f20c7c3c80947d1b47bade8f998e705d6f411ae3f298ecde2.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $cot⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $cot⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡(-x)=-cot⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $cot⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡(-a+5\\pi ) & =cot⁡(-a+\\pi \\cdot 5) \\\\  & =cot⁡(-a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $cot⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡(-a) & =-cot⁡a \\\\  & =-(-7) \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $cot⁡(-a+5\\pi )=7.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275440 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275053 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275440 1069 "q-158059" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275440 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275440 1071 "If $cos⁡x=0,$ then $\\pi cos⁡(-x+18\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275440 1072 #uuid "a595d8eb-425e-5670-9569-3c6cc685c869" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275440 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/23/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $cos⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric over the $y$-axis. This means that $cos⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡(-x)=cos⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $cos⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x+18\\pi ) & =cos⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 9) \\\\  & =cos⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $cos⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x) & =cos⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pi cos⁡(-x+18\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pi \\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275441 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275007 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275441 1069 "q-15806" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275441 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275441 1071 "![](images/43/43fe48b1980d01aa9dbb7cc0f614272ad19e7cfe47b5a5a83a0f81e7539f285b.png)\n\nFind the points where the circle $(x-2)^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=10$ intersects the line $3y-x=-7.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275441 1072 #uuid "617f695b-fb85-5dd2-a74d-894ae9304fcc" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275441 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation $3y-x=-7,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+(y+5)^{2} & =10 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}+5)}^{2} & =10 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{8}{3})}^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{16x}{9}+\\frac{64}{9} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{10{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{20x}{9}+\\frac{100}{9} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+10 & =9 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $x=1$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line $3y-x=-7$ intersects the circle $(x-2)^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=10$ at the point $(1,-2).$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275442 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274716 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275442 1069 "q-158064" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275442 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275442 1071 "If $cot⁡x=-6,$ then $\\frac{1}{2}cot⁡(-x+15\\pi )+7=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275442 1072 #uuid "2a79aaf7-1dcc-57ab-a0b7-6d93a2b468da" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275442 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cot⁡x.$\n\n![](images/6e/6eb672fe024f024f20c7c3c80947d1b47bade8f998e705d6f411ae3f298ecde2.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $cot⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $cot⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡(-x)=-cot⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $cot⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡(-x+15\\pi ) & =cot⁡(-x+\\pi \\cdot 15) \\\\  & =cot⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $cot⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡(-x) & =-cot⁡x \\\\  & =-(-6) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}cot⁡(-x+15\\pi )+7 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6+7 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2}+7 \\\\  & =3+7 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275443 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274995 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275443 1069 "q-158065" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275443 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275443 1071 "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡a=\\frac{11}{4},$ then $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-a)=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275443 1072 #uuid "990fed19-47aa-5e1c-b060-215e7bc1b3ed" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275443 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e7894ea970c539ca59a73713fa15450f4361b3fc8bc22183e3fe84946411bd.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-a) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡a \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275444 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275075 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275444 1069 "q-158066" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275444 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275444 1071 "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡a=\\sqrt{5},$ then $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-a)=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275444 1072 #uuid "bd48781b-b5c4-5134-ac43-371c5870ba80" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275444 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e7894ea970c539ca59a73713fa15450f4361b3fc8bc22183e3fe84946411bd.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-a) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡a \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275445 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274937 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275445 1069 "q-158069" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275445 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275445 1071 "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-4,$ then $3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-18\\pi )-7=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275445 1072 #uuid "7d2f0215-ecc5-5f39-91e6-424fd801bf23" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275445 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e7894ea970c539ca59a73713fa15450f4361b3fc8bc22183e3fe84946411bd.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-18\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-2\\pi \\cdot 9) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\  & =-(-4) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-18\\pi )-7 & =3(4)-7 \\\\  & =12-7 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275446 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274998 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275446 1069 "q-158071" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275446 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275446 1071 "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{7}{6},$ then $4\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+6\\pi )+1=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275446 1072 #uuid "52fb1c00-a770-5c09-ab79-cdf019b59f0c" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275446 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e7894ea970c539ca59a73713fa15450f4361b3fc8bc22183e3fe84946411bd.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+6\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+6\\pi )+1 & =4(-\\frac{7}{6})+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{14}{3}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275447 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274818 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275447 1069 "q-158100" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275447 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275447 1071 "![](images/00/003b4f77a413444989bcec8b9682465419a58ded55ed95cefb4f43c122bdc3d3.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275447 1072 #uuid "0609c9a8-63cb-5946-854d-790ce4ad14b1" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275447 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents the origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275448 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274838 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275448 1069 "q-158101" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275448 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275448 1071 "![](images/27/2739a0225640ff20cc9678edf8c41ba45ff637b17a40d0322ca792bef1346f06.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{3}?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275448 1072 #uuid "7ce01e6e-4d84-5a03-940c-8de7146f3c76" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275448 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a line passing through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275449 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274698 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275449 1069 "q-158102" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275449 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275449 1071 "![](images/42/42b23af4df7892df46461b01fd549ca56723dde0c08d3e88b71ea119bf45ba23.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{3}?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275449 1072 #uuid "ec726d7a-9c79-5a1b-91e2-01dc3e4c2650" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275449 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a plane that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents the origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275450 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275014 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275450 1069 "q-158104" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275450 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275450 1071 "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275450 1072 #uuid "210e9083-358f-508e-9376-e356564e0e4d" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275450 1074 "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 7 & 13 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 7 & 13 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 2=24.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|24|=24.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275451 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274677 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275451 1069 "q-158106" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275451 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275451 1071 "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -11\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275451 1072 #uuid "edcd069d-5898-5a9f-9b4f-3515e782b5c1" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275451 1074 "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 8 & -11 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 8 & -11 & -2\\end{aligned}]=3\\cdot 3\\cdot 4\\cdot (-2)=-72.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-72|=72.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275452 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274682 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275452 1069 "q-158108" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275452 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275452 1071 "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275452 1072 #uuid "32e6e8c1-9b26-5825-ac5f-18dfd683a674" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275452 1074 "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]=7\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 5=-105.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-105|=105.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275453 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274786 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275453 1069 "q-158109" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275453 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275453 1071 "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275453 1072 #uuid "33a0a4e7-70bf-5d4f-8473-1e3ebaad90ce" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275453 1074 "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 7 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 7 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]=(-3)\\cdot (-5)\\cdot 4\\cdot 3=180.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|180|=180.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275454 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274659 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275454 1069 "q-158110" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275454 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275454 1071 "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275454 1072 #uuid "b4e39b2b-6ddb-522a-a369-dfb16a50311d" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275454 1074 "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 12 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 12 & 5\\end{aligned}]=6\\cdot 2\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 5=-180.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-180|=180.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275455 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275081 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275455 1069 "q-158114" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275455 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275455 1071 "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275455 1072 #uuid "c3a8456a-5f67-54bf-8d0a-07a8bbe82bf5" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275455 1074 "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $2$nd row of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $3$rd row of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =-2|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-6|\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-6(4-25) \\\\  & =126.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|126|=126.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275456 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274901 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275456 1069 "q-158116" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275456 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275456 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{sec}⁡y\\operatorname{tan}⁡y}$ for $x\\ne 0$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<y<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275456 1072 #uuid "b0b5140d-8021-52c8-9880-109bb8e78b6b" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275456 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{sec}⁡y\\operatorname{tan}⁡y} \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡y\\operatorname{tan}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡y\\operatorname{tan}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡y\\operatorname{tan}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡y\\operatorname{tan}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275457 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275019 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275457 1069 "q-158117" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275457 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275457 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ for $y\\ne 0.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275457 1072 #uuid "1c9d63db-85c4-5f46-a91d-0a91ded8b499" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275457 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275458 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274709 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275458 1069 "q-158124" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275458 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275458 1071 "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275458 1072 #uuid "58326646-892e-5c31-8f2b-b027f89873ae" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275458 1074 "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}34 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{38}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 34\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275459 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274857 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275459 1069 "q-158179" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275459 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275459 1071 "Find the domain of the function $z=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}.$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275459 1072 #uuid "8022d60c-ad02-52ea-a2bc-6bcc108f5f02" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275459 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1}.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275460 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274959 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275460 1069 "q-158192" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275460 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275460 1071 "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275460 1072 #uuid "2b882cc3-ef39-5506-bf91-1860550bd797" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275460 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the square root is non-negative, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,{y}^{2}<{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,-|x|<y<|x|\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-|x|<y<|x|}.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves the $<$ sign, the curves are *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the dashed lines $y=-|x|$ and $y=|x|,$ and shade the area *below* the curve $y=|x|,$ and *above* the curve $y=-|x|.$\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/c0/c06082999e1737b6e4ea5d32a3d8d77a271e3db49c38cc3725422bcb979676d0.png)" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275461 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274874 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275461 1069 "q-158193" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275461 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275461 1071 "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275461 1072 #uuid "59f09d6c-7a4e-5350-aa9e-955b620390c4" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275461 1074 "The function $f(x,y)$ is defined for all points $(x,y)$ on the plane, hence the domain is the entire $xy$-plane. \n\n![](images/40/402d90507403eabf4cd35cde83a45c9fa2d8080347e1debb0e2463be408b489b.png)" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275462 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274852 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275462 1069 "q-158198" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275462 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275462 1071 "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}?$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275462 1072 #uuid "a90460ca-8a09-5a2d-bb95-26ae2a0e2a46" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275462 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,{y}^{2}\\le {x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,-|x|\\le y\\le |x|\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-|x|\\le y\\le |x|}.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves the $\\le $ sign, the curves are part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the solid curves $y=-|x|$ and $y=|x|,$ and shade the area *below* the curve $y=|x|,$ and *above* the curve $y=-|x|.$\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/95/95f187d2857973e4fea83eedf47bd54c153ce2a77f278cfb992b222de2d91ab8.png)" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275463 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274860 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275463 1069 "q-1583" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275463 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275463 1071 "$\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡A}-\\frac{1}{{cot}^{2}⁡A}=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275463 1072 #uuid "d8d55196-4015-578e-afc9-4c54361316e6" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275463 1074 "First, we use the definition of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡A$ to rewrite the second term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡A}-\\frac{1}{{cot}^{2}⁡A} & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡A}-{tan}^{2}⁡A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡A}-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡A}{{cos}^{2}⁡A} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡A}{{cos}^{2}⁡A}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the identity $1-{sin}^{2}⁡A={cos}^{2}⁡A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡A}{{cos}^{2}⁡A} & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡A}{{cos}^{2}⁡A}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡A}-\\frac{1}{{cot}^{2}⁡A}=1\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275464 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274875 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275464 1069 "q-158304" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275464 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275464 1071 "Compute $P(X\\le -1)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{35}{x}^{2},\\, & -2\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275464 1072 #uuid "e86e99cd-7981-5bc4-8657-89323bada738" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275464 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a\\le X\\le b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is identical to zero for all $x<-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le -1) & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{3}{35}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{35}{x}^{3}]}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{35}[(-1)^{3}-(-2)^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{35}(-1+8) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{35}\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275465 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275037 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275465 1069 "q-158309" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275465 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275465 1071 "Compute $P(X\\le 3)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{24}({x}^{2}+1),\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275465 1072 #uuid "93f58db6-2e5f-58ae-a9a5-9aa2f152a653" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275465 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a\\le X\\le b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is identical to zero for all $x<1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{24}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+x]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}[(\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3}+3)-(\\frac{1}{3}+1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}(12-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot \\frac{32}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275466 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274911 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275466 1069 "q-158310" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275466 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275466 1071 "Compute $P(X>-3)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{72}{x}^{2},\\, & -6\\le x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275466 1072 #uuid "93973542-05ae-5f66-9567-d5a70adf747f" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275466 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X\\le b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is identical to zero for all $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>-3) & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}\\frac{1}{72}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{216}{x}^{3}]}_{-3}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{216}[(0)^{3}-(-3)^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{216} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275467 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275051 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275467 1069 "q-158353" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275467 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275467 1071 "Compute $P(1<X<2)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{20}{x}^{3},\\, & 1\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275467 1072 #uuid "8b51790a-125a-5ea1-9c27-c5d84bf2d46e" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275467 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<X<2) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{20}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{80}{x}^{4}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{80}(16-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275468 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274708 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275468 1069 "q-158357" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275468 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275468 1071 "Compute $P(X>1)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{27}(3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275468 1072 #uuid "8721f1b5-2f29-5e13-b09b-d65e858115c3" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275468 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X\\le b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is identical to zero for all $x>3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>1) & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{4}{27}(3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{27}{\\int }_{1}^{3}3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{27}{[{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{27}([(3)^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}(3)^{4}]-[(1)^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}(1)^{4}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{27}(27-\\frac{81}{4}-1+\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{27}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275469 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274762 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275469 1069 "q-158359" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275469 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275469 1071 "Find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane  $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275469 1072 #uuid "34228c5f-fec0-5af1-abb9-8c8570cc3eda" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275469 1074 "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}14 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275470 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275001 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275470 1069 "q-158360" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275470 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275470 1071 "Compute $P(X>-1)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{28}({x}^{2}+1),\\, & -2\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275470 1072 #uuid "23f23f15-f4eb-5059-a399-9f02804b0be1" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275470 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X\\le b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is identical to zero for all $x>2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>-1) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\frac{3}{28}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x]}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}([\\frac{1}{3}(2)^{3}+(2)]-[\\frac{1}{3}(-1)^{3}+(-1)]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}([\\frac{8}{3}+2]-[-\\frac{1}{3}-1]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275471 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274834 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275471 1069 "q-158363" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275471 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275471 1071 "Compute $P(X\\le 2)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{72}({x}^{3}+x),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275471 1072 #uuid "edf8c68d-b48c-5470-88a8-57b4271b4291" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275471 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a\\le X\\le b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is identical to zero for all $x<0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{72}({x}^{3}+x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{72}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}+x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{72}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{72}([\\frac{1}{4}(2)^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2}]-[\\frac{1}{4}(0)^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{72}(4+2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{72} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275472 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274832 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275472 1069 "q-158364" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275472 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275472 1071 "Compute $P(2<X\\le 3)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{21}{x}^{2},\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275472 1072 #uuid "e9b04154-2be7-58e1-9bc0-b47e870f4bfd" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275472 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X\\le b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X\\le 3) & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{21}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{63}{x}^{3}]}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{63}(27-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{63}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275473 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274974 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275473 1069 "q-158365" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275473 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275473 1071 "Compute $P(-1<X\\le 0)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9}(x+2)(1-x),\\, & -2\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275473 1072 #uuid "fb210de0-88ab-5a1a-9e08-ad52b67bfe25" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275473 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X\\le b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1<X\\le 0) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{2}{9}(x+2)(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{[-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+2x]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}([-\\frac{1}{3}(0)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}+2(0)]-[-\\frac{1}{3}(-1)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}+2(-1)]) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}(0-[\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}-2]) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{13}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275474 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274788 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275474 1069 "q-158368" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275474 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275474 1071 "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275474 1072 #uuid "c594e512-d19a-53c5-8f50-4caaf295111c" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275474 1074 "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $3$rd column of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =3|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 4\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-120\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-120|=120.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275475 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274813 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275475 1069 "q-158369" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275475 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275475 1071 "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275475 1072 #uuid "5d7bd54b-d293-5ee2-b790-9af4e0cb0ced" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275475 1074 "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]=3\\cdot 5\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 3=-45.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-45|=45.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275476 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274714 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275476 1069 "q-158371" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275476 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275476 1071 "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275476 1072 #uuid "94fb05dd-7b6c-5fef-becd-0abc3ccf0dc9" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275476 1074 "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 7 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $1$st row of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 7 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $2$nd row of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =-2|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 4\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 10 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =8|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 10 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =8(-14+30) \\\\  & =128.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|128|=128.\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275477 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274773 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275477 1069 "q-1584" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275477 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275477 1071 "$\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t-1}{{cos}^{2}⁡t{tan}^{2}⁡t}=$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275477 1072 #uuid "75e7ea31-6bb1-5da3-8967-5b9dbf480524" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275477 1074 "First, we rewrite the numerator using $1-{cos}^{2}⁡t={sin}^{2}⁡t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t-1}{{cos}^{2}⁡t{tan}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{-1\\cdot (1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{{cos}^{2}⁡t{tan}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the denominator using the definition of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta $ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{-{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot {(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot (\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{cos}^{2}⁡t})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{sin}^{2}⁡t}{(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡t}{{cos}^{2}⁡t})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{sin}^{2}⁡t}{(\\frac{\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡t}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡t}}{\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡t}} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t-1}{{cos}^{2}⁡t{tan}^{2}⁡t}=-1\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274798 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275478 1069 "q-158414" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275478 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275478 1071 "![](images/49/495b8aaac849e90ffd46f34f35dd4d3783c2c4fa35c3887c339387d31215d7af.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{4},\\, & 0<x<2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275478 1072 #uuid "0f120679-716b-5edd-8658-50352ee4a1a6" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275478 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x+1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}+2]-[\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+0]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4-0) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275479 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274997 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275479 1069 "q-158415" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275479 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275479 1071 "Compute $P(2<X\\le 3)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}x,\\, & 1\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 3<x\\le 4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275479 1072 #uuid "5d8a5e34-ca97-5251-9920-739d77c157ef" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275479 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X\\le b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X\\le 3) & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{6}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{2}]}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[{3}^{2}-{2}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(9-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275480 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274884 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275480 1069 "q-158416" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275480 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275480 1071 "Compute $P(0\\le X<1)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{3},\\, & 0\\le x<2, \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}x & 2\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275480 1072 #uuid "0311ab9a-9f38-5bbc-a13c-5b492913363c" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275480 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a\\le X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X<1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}({1}^{4}-{0}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275481 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274994 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275481 1069 "q-158418" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275481 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275481 1071 "Compute $P(2\\le X\\le 3)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{3}-x),\\, & 1\\le x<2, \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}(x-1) & 2\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275481 1072 #uuid "58f9edce-1e4c-5231-8a4d-c34dae703f86" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275481 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a\\le X\\le b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2\\le X\\le 3) & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{6}(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x]}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}([\\frac{1}{2}(3)^{2}-(3)]-[\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2}-2]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\frac{9}{2}-3-2+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275482 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274686 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275482 1069 "q-158419" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275482 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275482 1071 "![](images/4a/4acac98ef7276cb1e90557ca161666a33ccf0a7627b9ebfcbbe44508b9e5f5bb.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2},\\, & 0<x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275482 1072 #uuid "0ced5dff-e7df-54bc-a45c-2aa3418fd883" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275482 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{1}^{3}}{2}]-[\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{0}^{3}}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275483 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275035 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275483 1069 "q-158420" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275483 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275483 1071 "Compute $P(3<X<4)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2},\\, & -1\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}(x-1) & 1<x\\le 4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275483 1072 #uuid "1fa2bcf9-069e-5cbf-8261-5732b1e8e1ad" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true] [17592186275483 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<X<4) & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x]}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}([\\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2}-(4)]-[\\frac{1}{2}(3)^{2}-(3)]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(8-4-\\frac{9}{2}+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(\\frac{5}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533403 17592186045585 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0003a375-681f-5467-ad7f-0e316d8ea2ba" 17592186272393 "answer-0008a7ab-2b00-5ba5-82d0-3f2e6b8bdaa2" 17592186272394 "answer-0082e7c0-3530-59f2-ba9a-26fd87e6a277" 17592186272395 "answer-00bacbcd-590d-557f-a9f4-6a4269e68310" 17592186272396 "answer-00f7fd53-ae8f-5913-86fa-0ae247f81b2b" 17592186272397 "answer-0102fe79-93e9-5ba1-b296-a0bd93e787fe" 17592186272398 "answer-014a2989-c11d-5a73-96a0-2615550b05a6" 17592186272399 "answer-0161ff6a-e88e-5667-9f0e-5d0932fcb766" 17592186272400 "answer-019119ed-0828-5d14-913a-ecb47950b217" 17592186272401 "answer-01ade8ce-19e0-5958-a1b6-b246f43b7b08" 17592186272402 "answer-01b03335-77aa-59fa-b7e8-6e2269d546b0" 17592186272403 "answer-01b271c8-930d-55d2-b6c9-4d10415d1f40" 17592186272404 "answer-01ebf211-8fd3-5d62-86cd-f2533a48899a" 17592186272405 "answer-0206b020-8acc-5674-8515-83514e7ac110" 17592186272406 "answer-02463175-d734-5148-a9e9-8950cdeb6b02" 17592186272407 "answer-025d6b48-01e5-5f01-8d65-c71247ac2c9d" 17592186272408 "answer-02692810-69c5-51d1-8b75-972adfa093a2" 17592186272409 "answer-026f4ff4-a407-5899-a71e-ea73b388819c" 17592186272410 "answer-02713a78-db0c-5c55-afb6-d2b4992a0031" 17592186272411 "answer-029afac2